-- 0034_drill_corpus_v2_data.sql
--
-- v2 corpus fill — 85 sourced drills bringing the corpus 40 → 125 across
-- all 6 practice modes. Mirrors the seed.sql v2 fill committed in PR #444
-- (feature/drill-corpus-v2-fill). Each drill carries a live URL + named
-- PGA pro / teaching authority / reputable publication; every numeric/
-- attribution claim verified against the source.
--
-- Mode counts after: warmup 14 · blocked 44 · random 15 ✅ · skill_game 27 ✅
-- · pressure_game 15 ✅ · on_course 10 ✅
--
-- Idempotency: adds a UNIQUE constraint on drills.name (which the data
-- already satisfies — every corpus drill name is distinct) so the INSERT
-- can ON CONFLICT (name) DO NOTHING and is safe to re-run.

-- Make the INSERT idempotent on re-apply.
alter table public.drills add constraint drills_name_unique unique (name);

insert into public.drills (name, description, duration_min, category, facility, skill_levels, instructions, goals, targets, drill_type, source, source_url, contributor, verified) values


($drill$No-Repeat Club Rotation$drill$,
 $drill$Hit a different club on every shot — never the same club twice in a row — to interleave your whole bag the way an actual round forces you to.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Patrick Nuber's GOLFTEC technique attacks the single biggest flaw in how amateurs use a range: blocked repetition. Hitting twenty 7-irons in a row builds a groove that evaporates the moment you step onto the course, where you never hit the same club twice. The one rule — you cannot use the same club on consecutive shots — forces the brain to re-plan, re-select, and re-calibrate every single rep, which is exactly the retrieval work a round demands. Decades of motor-learning research (and Nuber's own teaching) show interleaved practice feels worse in the moment (more mishits) but transfers far better than blocked blocks. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who strike it well on the range but lose it on the course; casual players past basic contact who want their practice to actually count. Not ideal for raw beginners still grooving a repeatable strike. **Setup.** A range bay and your full bag. Pick a real course you play and call its holes in your head. **How.** Play an imaginary round: driver off the first tee with full intent and routine, then the club the result leaves you, then the next hole's tee shot — switching clubs every shot, never repeating back-to-back. Treat each as one shot that counts; no rakes of balls with the same club. Visualize the hole, pick a target, run your full pre-shot routine, then change clubs. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1–2×/week — the back half of a session once you've warmed up. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete 18 "holes" without repeating a club back-to-back, real routine on each. • Developing: hit your intended shape/target on 12 of 18. • Competitive: shot pattern on the range matches what you produce on the course — the gap closes. **Common mistakes.** Reverting to two-in-a-row with a favorite club; skipping the pre-shot routine to rush; not visualizing a real target (random clubs to no target is just noise); judging the session by total flush count instead of plan-execute-adapt reps.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,course_management}', 'random',
 $drill$Patrick Nuber (GOLFTEC)$drill$, 'https://scramble.golftec.com/blog/2016/07/golf-instruction-video-random-practice-technique/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Nine-Shot Cycle (Driver)$drill$,
 $drill$Work the full 3×3 window — low, medium, high crossed with draw, straight, fade — so you own every tee-shot shape instead of owning only one.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tyler Ferrell teaches the nine-shot matrix — three trajectories (low/medium/high) times three shapes (draw/straight/fade) — as the most complete test of clubface and path control: low draw, low straight, low fade, then the medium row, then the high row. Producing a shot on demand proves you control the variables rather than the variables controlling you; Ferrell controls trajectory mainly through ball position (back = lower, draw bias; forward = higher, fade bias) layered onto path and face. **We apply Ferrell's nine-shot framework specifically to the driver** — the club where amateurs are most one-dimensional and most punished for a single stock miss. Cycling all nine builds the adaptability to flight one down into the wind or hold a fade against a left pin off the tee. **Who it helps most.** Developing players whose driver has exactly one shape (and one miss) and competitive players widening their tee-shot options. Not for beginners — it assumes a repeatable strike. **Setup.** Range, driver, an alignment aid for start line. Tee height stays normal; vary ball position deliberately. **How.** Hit the nine in order — low draw, low straight, low fade, medium draw/straight/fade, high draw/straight/fade — one ball each, naming the shot before you hit it. Easiest combos are low draw and high fade; hardest are low fade and high draw, so don't be discouraged by those. Two cycles. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week on shaping. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clearly produce the intended trajectory on 6 of 9. • Competitive: hit 8 of 9 the called shape and start line; nail the two hard corners (low fade, high draw). **Common mistakes.** Trying to shape only with the hands instead of ball position + path; abandoning a shot after one bad rep; no defined start-line reference; forcing huge curves (tour shapes are subtle — a usable fade is yards, not a slice).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,trajectory,dispersion}', 'random',
 $drill$Tyler Ferrell (Golf Smart Academy); driver application by OGA$drill$, 'https://golfsmartacademy.com/golf-instruction/9-shot-drill', 'oga', true),

($drill$Five Flights in a Minute$drill$,
 $drill$Hit five different ball flights inside one minute — fade, draw, slice, hook, straight — all to one target, so the clock stops you from over-thinking mechanics.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Adam Young's drill is deliberate variability under time pressure: in about a minute you hit five distinct flights — a fade, a draw, a bigger fade (slice), a bigger draw (hook), and a straight ball — every one finishing within roughly ten yards of the same target. The constraint does two things. First, variety: producing opposite curves back to back proves you control face-to-path rather than guessing. Second, the one-minute clock kills paralysis-by-analysis — there's no time to manipulate positions, so the swing self-organizes around the *intention* of the flight. Young's coaching point is to focus only on how the club moves through impact (path direction plus face-to-path) and let the body figure out the rest. **Who it helps most.** Developing players stuck on one shape who freeze when asked to curve it the other way; competitive players sharpening on-demand shot-making and pre-round feel. **Setup.** Range, one club to start (a mid-iron is easiest; progress to driver), a single target, a watch or just a quick count. **How.** Pick the target. In one minute, hit in order: fade, draw, slice, hook, straight — each landing within ~10 yards of the target line-appropriate spot. Swing-left-with-an-open-face biases a fade; swing-right-with-a-closed-face biases a draw; exaggerate for the slice and hook. Don't reset between shots — keep moving. Rest, repeat 3–4 rounds. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 2×/week; a great pre-round feel primer. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: produce all five recognizable flights within the minute, even if dispersion is loose. • Competitive: all five within ~10 yards of target and clearly the called curve. **Common mistakes.** Going so slow the time pressure (the point) disappears; manipulating the hands instead of changing path/face intent; chasing perfect contact over producing the shape; never progressing from iron to driver.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,trajectory}', 'random',
 $drill$Adam Young$drill$, 'https://adamyounggolf.com/shot-shaping-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Draw/Fade/Straight Scorecard$drill$,
 $drill$Score a twelve-ball shape test — a draw, a fade, and a straight ball in every batch of three — and chase the handicap benchmark to put pressure on your shot-shaping.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** This GOLF.com game from Top 100 Teacher Jason Baile turns shot-shaping into a scored, repeatable test, which adds the pressure that range balls normally lack. You hit twelve balls in four batches of three; in each batch you hit a draw, then a fade, then a straight shot, in that order. One point for every shot that finishes inside a reasonable margin of its intended start line, for a maximum of twelve. Scoring forces commitment — you can't bail on a shape mid-swing without losing a point — and rotating the called shape every shot is true random practice. The driver is the hardest club to do this with, so it's the most honest test of tee-shot control. **Who it helps most.** Any player who wants a number to chase; developing players proving they can curve it both ways; competitive players benchmarking. **Setup.** Range, a target with an intermediate marker on the line, any club from mid-iron to driver (driver is hardest). **How.** Place a marker between ball and target for a start-line reference. Hit 4 batches of 3 — draw, fade, straight each batch. Award 1 point per shot that starts on its intended line with the intended curve; track the total out of 12. **Benchmark with a driver (from the source):** single-digit handicaps aim for 8, mid-handicaps 6, high-handicaps 4. Beat your number next time. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly; a fun way to end a driver session. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hit your handicap benchmark (≈4) and identify which shape is your weak one. • Developing: 6+ and both curves usable. • Competitive: 8+ with a driver, repeatably. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the start-line marker so scoring is guesswork; only scoring whether it went straight (the called curve matters); decelerating on the uncomfortable shape; treating it as one big bucket instead of committed, scored shots.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,dispersion}', 'random',
 $drill$Jason Baile, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com / Luke Kerr-Dineen)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/fun-driving-range-game-improve-driving-accuracy/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Random-Club, New-Target$drill$,
 $drill$Change both the club and the target on every single shot — pick a club at random, pick a target that fits it, full routine, repeat — the research-backed way to make range reps transfer.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** This is the cleanest statement of random practice for the approach game, from Eric Alpenfels (GOLF Top 100 Teacher) and Dr. Bob Christina of "Evidence-Based Golf." Their controlled studies found that golfers who randomized club and target every shot transferred their skills to the course markedly better than golfers who hammered the same shot in blocks — even though the random group looked worse during practice. The mechanism is retrieval: every shot you reconstruct the plan from scratch (read the number, pick the club, pick the target, commit), which is precisely the cognitive task a real approach shot requires. Blocked practice removes that step and so removes the transfer. **Who it helps most.** Every level past raw beginner; especially players who "own the range" but scatter approaches on the course. **Setup.** Range with several visible target greens/flags at different distances, your full set of irons and wedges. **How.** Pick a club at random (literally grab one without looking, or rotate unpredictably), choose a target appropriate for that club, run your full pre-shot routine, hit one shot, then change BOTH club and target for the next. Never two in a row the same. No raking balls. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, every approach session — make it your default range mode. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete 15 shots, full routine each, no repeats. • Developing: finish within a green's width of target on 9 of 15 across mixed clubs. • Competitive: dispersion holds across the bag — no club is a "surprise." **Common mistakes.** Drifting back into a comfortable club; choosing a target that doesn't match the club; skipping the routine to save time (the routine is the transferable part); judging by strike quality instead of plan-and-execute reps.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,contact,dispersion}', 'random',
 $drill$Eric Alpenfels & Dr. Bob Christina, Evidence-Based Golf (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/10-proven-ways-make-range-practice-stick/', 'oga', true),

($drill$TrackMan Combine (Approach)$drill$,
 $drill$Take TrackMan's sixty-shot assessment — ten target distances, a new distance every three shots, scored 0–100 on carry — to benchmark your iron game against real tour data.$drill$,
 45, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The TrackMan Combine is the standardized assessment tour players and coaches use to put an objective number on iron and wedge precision. You hit 60 shots to 10 target distances; the distance changes every 3 shots, so you can never settle into a groove — it is random practice by design and a true test of on-demand distance control. Each shot is scored on how close its carry lands to the target, producing a 0–100 score with handicap-style ratings that vary by distance band, and the whole thing is repeatable so you can track progress over weeks. The honesty of a launch monitor (carry, not where it bounced) removes the self-flattery of eyeballing the range. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with access to a launch monitor who want a measured baseline and a number to improve; less useful for beginners still building a strike. **Requires a launch monitor** (a TrackMan, or any monitor that reports carry — facility hint: range or sim). **Setup.** A launch monitor set to a Combine or manual distance protocol, your irons/wedges, ~45 minutes and a bucket. **How.** Run the 10 target distances (typically 60–160+ yards depending on the protocol), 6 shots at each in the official Combine, or this approach-focused cut of 3 shots per distance with the distance rotating each time. Record the 0–100 score; note which distance bands score worst — that's your practice target. **Time & frequency.** 45 minutes; monthly as a benchmark, not a daily grind. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete a full Combine and establish a baseline score. • Competitive: improve the baseline over time; close the gap on your two weakest distance bands. **Common mistakes.** Treating it as a hit-fest instead of one committed shot per rep; only chasing the total instead of reading the per-distance breakdown; skipping the routine because a screen is watching; comparing raw scores across different distance protocols.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,dispersion}', 'random',
 $drill$TrackMan (TrackMan Combine), via GolfCave$drill$, 'https://golfcave.com/blog/trackman-combine', 'oga', true),

($drill$Throw-and-Play Darts Scatter$drill$,
 $drill$Toss six balls around the green and play each from wherever it lands into a four-foot circle — random lies you didn't choose, exactly like the course.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Fellow Ben Emerson's scatter drill solves the problem with most short-game practice: dropping six balls in one tidy spot and chipping them all the same way. On the course you almost never get a clean, flat, chosen lie — you get whatever the ball found. Throwing the balls out and playing them from wherever they come to rest (no improving the lie) reproduces that randomness: a different distance, slope, lie, and shot selection on every rep. The four-foot scoring circle around the hole gives an honest, beat-your-best target without pretending every chip should hole out. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the random lie is the great equalizer; especially players whose practice chips look great but who get fooled on the course by an awkward lie. **Setup.** A short-game green, 6 balls, your wedge(s). Pick a hole and picture a 4-foot circle around it. **How.** Stand near the green and toss the 6 balls out to random positions around it — short side, long side, in the fringe, in the rough. Play each ball from exactly where it lies; do not nudge it to a better spot or re-tee. Read the lie, pick the shot (chip, pitch, flop), and try to finish inside the 4-foot circle. Count how many of 6 finish inside; do it once and try to beat it next session. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; great late in a short-game session once you're warm. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 2 of 6 inside the circle. • Developing: 3–4 of 6. • Competitive: 5 of 6, from genuinely awkward lies. **Common mistakes.** Secretly improving the lie; throwing all 6 to similar easy spots; using only one club when the lie calls for a different shot; quitting and re-dropping after a bad one instead of accepting the random lie.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'random',
 $drill$Ben Emerson, PGA Fellow (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/4-chipping-drills-guaranteed-lower-scores-178437', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tiger Pin Rotation$drill$,
 $drill$Run Tiger's wedge warm-up — three chips each to short, long, and middle pins with two wedges, then a short-sided shot over a bunker — rotating club and target so no two chips repeat.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods's short-game warm-up, as documented by GOLF.com, is a model of rotating, never-repeat practice rather than a scored game. He chips in groups of three to constantly changing pins: with a 56° wedge, three chips to a short pin, three longer chips, three to a middle pin; then switches to a 60° wedge for short and long pins; then finishes with a short-sided shot played over a bunker. Every group changes the distance, trajectory, or club, so the feel resets each time — the same demand the course makes. The progression also walks through the trajectory ladder (lower running chips up to higher, softer shots) and finishes with the hardest, most pressure-packed shot, the short-sider over sand. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who want a structured, tour-tested feel-builder and a pre-round routine; it assumes solid contact. *(This is a rotation routine, not a scored game — there is no points system.)* **Setup.** A short-game green with multiple pin options and ideally a greenside bunker, a 56° and a 60° wedge (or your two highest-lofted wedges), a small bucket. **How.** 56° wedge: 3 chips to a short pin, 3 to a long pin, 3 to a middle pin. 60° wedge: 3 to a short pin, 3 to a long pin. Finish: a handful of short-sided shots played over the bunker to a tight pin. Read each shot fresh; don't groove one length. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes; an excellent pre-round warm-up and a 2×/week feel session. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: land each group on the intended trajectory and within a few feet of the pin most of the time. • Competitive: control trajectory across both wedges and get the short-sided shots to settle quickly. **Common mistakes.** Inventing a scoring system the routine doesn't have and grinding on it; hitting more than three before rotating (defeats the reset); skipping the short-sided finisher because it's hard (it's the point); using only one wedge.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,trajectory,distance_control}', 'random',
 $drill$Tiger Woods (Golf.com / Luke Kerr-Dineen)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/tiger-woods-warm-up-practice-routine/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Up-and-Down Ladder$drill$,
 $drill$Climb a five-to-seven-rung up-and-down ladder — each made chip-and-putt moves you to a harder spot, each miss drops you back — so progress is never guaranteed and the pressure builds.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Nick Foy's ladder turns scattered short-game reps into a progressive, pressure-tested challenge. You set 5 to 7 stations of increasing difficulty (longer, tighter, worse lies, short-sided) and must get up-and-down — chip plus one putt — to advance a rung; a miss drops you back down. Because you can only climb by completing the full up-and-down, the drill scores what actually matters on the course (saving par from around the green) instead of just chip quality, and the climb-or-fall mechanic injects real consequence. The variety across rungs makes it random practice: every rung is a different shot. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who lose strokes failing to convert greenside chances; players whose chips are fine but whose comeback putts leak. **Setup.** A short-game green with a hole, a wedge, and a putter. Pick 5–7 spots around the green ordered easiest to hardest. **How.** Start at rung 1. Chip, then putt out. Make the up-and-down (holed in two or fewer) and advance to the next, harder rung; fail and drop back one rung. Goal: reach the top rung, or complete the whole ladder in the fewest total attempts, or hit the top three times. Track attempts and beat it next time. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 2×/week on scoring. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: reach the top rung within a session. • Competitive: top the ladder in a low attempt count, or three clean ascents. **Common mistakes.** Conceding the comeback putt instead of holing it (the putt is half the test); setting all rungs at similar difficulty; abandoning the drop-back rule when frustrated; not tracking attempts so there's nothing to beat.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,short_putts,distance_control}', 'random',
 $drill$Nick Foy (Foy Golf Academy)$drill$, 'https://foygolfacademy.com/best-chipping-games', 'oga', true),

($drill$Dollar Signs Scatter$drill$,
 $drill$Chip from five scattered spots, three balls each, scoring fake money — +$10 holed, +$2 inside three feet, -$5 off the green — and try to leave the green in profit.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** This Partee of 18 game adds stakes to short-game practice through a money-scoring system that rewards aggression where it pays and punishes the mistakes that wreck a scorecard. You play five different spots around the green, three balls from each, and bank: +$10 for holing out, +$2 for finishing inside three feet, -$5 for missing the green entirely, and break-even otherwise. The scoring teaches real short-game decision-making — a chip-in is worth chasing, but spraying one off the green is a disaster — and rotating five spots with three balls each keeps the lies and distances varied. It works solo (beat your previous bankroll) or as a competition. **Who it helps most.** Every level; particularly players who either bail out too cautiously or take dumb risks around the green. **Setup.** A short-game green with a hole, 15 balls (or replay 3 per spot), a wedge. Choose 5 distinct spots — different distances, lies, and sides. **How.** From spot 1, chip 3 balls and tally the money. Move to spots 2–5, 3 balls each (15 shots total). Add it up: the goal is to finish in the black, ideally with a few +$10 hole-outs. Record your bankroll and try to beat it. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly; fun to run with a practice partner for stakes. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: finish at break-even or better; no off-green -$5 penalties. • Developing: positive bankroll with at least a couple inside-3-feet. • Competitive: a strongly positive total with hole-outs. **Common mistakes.** Playing every shot from the same easy spot; chasing hole-outs so hard you rack up -$5 misses; not actually tallying (the scoring is the teaching tool); ignoring the lie and forcing one shot type.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'random',
 $drill$Partee of 18$drill$, 'https://parteeof18.com/blog/golf-chipping-games', 'oga', true),

($drill$Never-the-Same-Putt-Twice$drill$,
 $drill$Putt from 5, 10, and 15 feet at two holes on a slope, alternating uphill and downhill, so you never face the same putt twice — Phil Kenyon's on-course simulation.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Renowned putting coach Phil Kenyon designed this to replicate what actually happens on the greens: you never hit the same putt twice in a round, yet most players practice by raining balls at one hole from one spot. Set two hole locations on a sloping part of the green with tees at 5, 10, and 15 feet from each, and alternate uphill and downhill putts. Because every putt changes distance and direction, you read and feel each one fresh — Kenyon's exact point: "because you are never hitting the same putt twice, it replicates what happens on the course." The varied slope also trains the most fragile skill, speed control on breaking putts. **Who it helps most.** Every level past beginner; especially three-putt-prone players and those who putt great in practice but not in rounds. **Setup.** A sloping practice green, two holes, tees set at 5/10/15 feet from each, one ball (or a few). **How.** Work the distances and directions in a varied order — a 10-foot downhiller, then a 5-foot uphiller at the other hole, then a 15-footer across the slope — never repeating the same putt back to back. Kenyon uses safety zones to grade speed: leave a downhill putt inside about 2 feet past, an uphill putt inside about 1 foot past; advance on a make-or-safe, repeat the putt if you leave it short or blow it past the zone. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2–3×/week; doubles as pre-round speed calibration. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: finish inside the safety zone on most putts; no putts left short on uphillers. • Developing: hole the 5-footers and lag the 15-footers inside the zone consistently. • Competitive: control speed up and down the slope so comebacks are tap-ins. **Common mistakes.** Repeating the same putt to "get it" (defeats the design); only practicing uphill putts; ignoring the speed zones and judging only makes; setting both holes on a flat section so there's no break to read.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,green_reading}', 'random',
 $drill$Phil Kenyon (Today's Golfer)$drill$, 'https://todays-golfer.com/features/instruction-features/2020/september/phil-kenyon-golf-putting-tips/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Nine-in-a-Row, Different Break$drill$,
 $drill$Make nine three-footers in a row across three different holes and breaks — miss one and the streak resets — then push the tees back to four feet.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Channel instructor Ed Oldham's drill combines random practice with consecutive-make pressure. You place tees three feet from the hole at three different holes around the green, each with a different break (left-to-right, right-to-left, straight). Make one at each hole, loop back, and keep going — the goal is nine makes in a row, every putt a different hole and break. Oldham's point: "the goal is to make nine putts in a row, each putt at a different hole and with a different break." Constantly changing the break stops you from grooving one stroke, and the streak requirement manufactures the tightening pressure of a real short putt that matters. **Who it helps most.** Every level; especially players who miss short putts under pressure or who only practice dead-straight three-footers. **Setup.** A practice green with at least three holes on varied slope, tees set 3 feet out at each, a few balls. **How.** Putt at hole 1, then hole 2, then hole 3 — different break each time — then cycle back. Keep a running count of consecutive makes; any miss resets you to zero. Reach 9 in a row, then move all the tees to 4 feet and start again. **Time & frequency.** 10–15 minutes, 2–3×/week; a great confidence finisher and pre-round routine. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 9 in a row at 3 feet. • Developing: 9 in a row at 4 feet. • Competitive: extend to 5+ feet, or require a longer streak. **Common mistakes.** Putting from the same line repeatedly instead of rotating breaks; giving yourself the short ones; rushing after a few makes; quitting the reset rule when the streak breaks late (the reset is where the pressure lives).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{short_putts,green_reading}', 'random',
 $drill$Ed Oldham (Golf Channel)$drill$, 'https://www.golfchannel.com/news/random-practice-golf', 'oga', true),

($drill$Random Tee-Peg Lag$drill$,
 $drill$Set five or six tees down a long putt line and lag each ball to rest at a peg — then call the pegs in random order to train distance control across changing lengths.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA coach Matthew Johns built this lag drill around discrete distance targets. Place 5 or 6 balls along a long putt line and tee each one up; the task is to roll your putt so it comes to rest right at the base of a chosen tee peg. Tee pegs are small, specific targets — far more demanding than "somewhere near the hole" — so they sharpen distance control fast. The random element is the key second phase: once you can rest the ball within a foot of a peg consistently, you start calling the pegs in random order, simulating the constantly varying lengths of real lag putts instead of a tidy progression. **Who it helps most.** Every level; especially players who three-putt from distance or leave long putts well short. **Setup.** A practice green with room for a 30–40-foot line, 5–6 balls, 5–6 tees pushed in along the line at increasing distances, a putter. **How.** Phase 1: putt to each peg in order, trying to leave the ball resting at the base of that peg; build until you're within about a foot consistently. Phase 2: call the pegs in random order — a far one, then a near one, then a middle — so every putt is a new distance you must judge cold. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week; ideal pre-round speed work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: finish within ~3 feet of the called peg. • Developing: within ~1 foot in random order. • Competitive: stop the ball within a foot of any called peg on demand, including the longest. **Common mistakes.** Staying in the easy in-order phase forever; using the hole instead of small tee targets (less precise feedback); decelerating on the long ones; not mixing the order in phase two.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'random',
 $drill$Matthew Johns, PGA (GolfMagic)$drill$, 'https://golfmagic.com/instruction/putting/golf-practice-drills-putting-distance-control/15674', 'oga', true),

($drill$Variable-Distance Clock$drill$,
 $drill$Keep the around-the-clock circle but make every putt a different length — two feet here, ten feet there — so you reset your speed read on every single putt.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF.com instructor Gia Liwski's variable take on the classic clock drill fixes the flaw in the fixed version: putting twelve identical-length putts around one hole grooves a single stroke length that doesn't transfer. Here you keep the circle-around-the-hole layout, but each station is a deliberately different distance — a 2-footer, then a 10-footer, then a 4-footer, then a 12-footer — so you re-calibrate speed and read on every putt the way the course demands. The circle still delivers every break direction (uphill, downhill, both sidehill), but the changing distance is what makes it random practice rather than a groove. **Who it helps most.** Every level; especially players who only practice one putt length and lose speed control when distance varies. *(Use the variable-distance version, not the fixed 3–5-foot circle — that one overlaps a straight short-putt drill.)* **Setup.** A practice green with break around one hole, 8–12 balls or tees set at varied distances (roughly 2–12 feet) around the circle, a putter. **How.** Place your markers at clearly different distances around the hole. Work around the circle hitting each putt once; because each is a new length and a new break direction, commit to a fresh read and speed every time. To add pressure, require finishing inside a tap-in radius on the long ones and holing the short ones; restart on a miss. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hole the short stations, lag the long ones close. • Developing: get all the way around with no three-putt-length leaves. • Competitive: complete the circle holing the short ones and stopping the long ones within a foot. **Common mistakes.** Setting all stations the same distance (that's the fixed clock — not this drill); only doing the short putts; ignoring break because you're focused on distance; not committing to a new speed each station.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,short_putts}', 'random',
 $drill$Gia Liwski (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/effective-putting-practice-routine-clock-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Predict-and-Score 9-Hole$drill$,
 $drill$Read, commit, and putt from 10, 15, and 20 feet — then call where the ball will finish before it stops and score your prediction, training feel and read together.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David MacKenzie's predict-and-score drill trains the skill that actually separates good lag putters: an accurate internal model of where the ball is going, not just whether it drops. You putt three balls each from 10, 15, and 20 feet (nine putts), and the scored task is to predict the finish — holed, short-right, long-left, and so on — and grade the prediction. MacKenzie's scoring rewards reading reality over getting lucky: holing it with a correct call scores top marks, holing it with a wrong call scores less, a correct directional read on a miss still scores, and a completely misjudged putt scores zero. *We run the eyes-open version* (call the finish before the ball stops rolling) to keep it distinct from blind, eyes-closed feel work — here the point is fusing your read and your speed judgment into one honest prediction. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players sharpening lag feel and green-reading; anyone who leaves long putts in three-putt range. **Setup.** A practice green with mild break, tees at 10/15/20 feet, 3 balls, something to track score. **How.** From each distance, take your full read, commit, and putt. As the ball rolls — before it stops — call where it will finish (holed / short / long / left / right). Then confirm and score: holed + correct call = 3, holed + wrong call = 2, correct directional read on a miss = up to 2, badly misjudged = 0. Total across 9 putts; beat your score next session. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: predictions match reality on 5 of 9. • Developing: 7 of 9, with lag putts finishing close. • Competitive: 8–9 of 9 and tap-in leaves from 20 feet. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the prediction (it becomes an ordinary lag drill); judging only by makes (an accurate read on a miss is the real lesson); always predicting "good"; not varying the read by committing to a fresh line each putt.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,green_reading}', 'random',
 $drill$David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind); eyes-open variant by OGA$drill$, 'https://golfstateofmind.com/one-of-the-best-putting-drills-for-increasing-feel/', 'oga', true),

($drill$HORSE Around the Green$drill$,
 $drill$A two-player chipping showdown — match your partner's called shot or take a letter; first to spell H-O-R-S-E loses. Needs a partner.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** This Partee of 18 take on basketball's HORSE turns short-game practice into a head-to-head game, and the competition is the point: a chip you "have to" get close to win a hole is a far better rehearsal of on-course pressure than a relaxed practice rep. One player picks a spot and a shot and plays it; if it's good, the opponent must match it from the same spot; whoever fails to match earns a letter, and the first to spell H-O-R-S-E loses. Because the leader chooses the spot and shot, the variety is built in — different lies, distances, and trajectories every hole — and you're forced to execute shots you wouldn't otherwise practice. *(This is a partner game; it can't be done solo.)* **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who practice together and want pressure; anyone whose short game is fine in practice but tightens up when it counts. **Setup.** A short-game green, two (or more) players, a wedge each, a ball each. Agree on the letters (H-O-R-S-E) and whether mulligans exist. **How.** Player A picks a spot and calls a shot (e.g., a high flop to a tight pin); if A pulls it off acceptably, Player B must play the same shot from the same spot. B failing to match earns a letter. Winner of the exchange sets the next shot (or alternate who leads). First to spell the word loses. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes whenever you practice with someone. **Success metric.** Win the match; track who wins over sessions. The deeper win is executing pressure shots — short-sided, awkward lie — you'd normally avoid. **Common mistakes.** Always calling your comfort shot; setting shots so hard nobody can match (no learning); skipping lies that scare you; treating it as casual so the pressure never appears.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Partee of 18$drill$, 'https://parteeof18.com/blog/golf-chipping-games', 'oga', true),

($drill$Crazy 3's$drill$,
 $drill$A two-player short-game contest — pick three tough spots, everyone chips all three, closest to the pin each time scores; most points wins. Needs a partner.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Partee of 18's Crazy 3's is a closest-to-the-pin competition that forces you to play three genuinely different short-game shots under the pressure of a head-to-head score. One player picks a pin, then chooses three difficult spots around the green; every player chips from all three, and the closest ball at each spot scores a point. Most points wins. Letting the leader pick hard, varied spots guarantees three distinct shots — a long-side runner, a short-sided flop, a shot from the rough — and the closest-to-the-pin scoring makes each one matter. *(This is a partner game; it can't be done solo.)* **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who practice together; players who need pressure to reveal which shots they don't actually own. **Setup.** A short-game green, two-plus players, wedges, a ball each. Pick a pin. **How.** The leader selects 3 challenging spots. Each player chips from spot 1; closest to the pin gets a point. Repeat for spots 2 and 3. Most points across the three spots wins; rotate who picks the spots between rounds so the shot variety keeps changing. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes whenever you practice with someone. **Success metric.** Win the round; over time, win from the spots you used to lose — the short-sided and rough lies. **Common mistakes.** Picking three similar easy spots; conceding "closest" without checking; only one player picking spots every round; not committing to the shot the spot demands because there's a score on the line.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Partee of 18$drill$, 'https://parteeof18.com/blog/golf-chipping-games', 'oga', true),


($drill$Sharp Shooter$drill$,
 $drill$Play a quiet round where each drive has to earn its reward — a fairway (or close) buys you a bonus approach ball; a wild one doesn't — so the tee shot finally carries on-course consequence.$drill$,
 90, 'off_tee', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Iain Highfield (KOHR Golf Academy; author of *Golf Practice*) built Sharp Shooter to kill the gap between range driving and course driving: on the range every tee shot is consequence-free, so you never rehearse committing under the outcome stress that actually wrecks drives on the course. Here, every drive matters. On a quiet course you hit one tee shot per hole; if it meets the standard you earn a "bonus ball" to play your approach from, then play the better of the two into the hole. The reward structure makes a good drive valuable in real time — exactly the pressure a normal range bucket can't manufacture — and it trains the scoring chain (drive → approach), not an isolated swing. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who strike it fine on the range but get tight off the first tee; the published standard is an intermediate one, so it assumes a player who can find fairways some of the time. Skip it for raw beginners (the bonus standard becomes unreachable and the lesson is lost). **Setup.** A real course at a quiet time (early/late), your normal bag, one ball played per tee shot plus a spare for the bonus. Agree your fairway standard up front: the published intermediate rule is the drive must finish **in the fairway or within one club-length of it** to earn the bonus. **How.** On each hole, hit one tee shot. If it meets the standard, drop a bonus ball at a forward approach position (50–90 yards from the green) and play both your real approach and the bonus, then continue with the better ball and hole out. If the drive misses the standard, no bonus — play your real ball as it lies. Score birdies made, or total strokes for 9 or 18, and try to beat it next time. **Time & frequency.** A 9- or 18-hole loop; whenever the course is quiet. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: earn the bonus on half your holes; beat your stroke total over a few attempts. • Competitive: earn the bonus on most holes and make several birdies. **Common mistakes.** Playing it when the course is busy (you'll hold people up); fudging the fairway standard so every drive "earns" the bonus; treating the bonus ball as the only ball that counts; doing it on a range (the whole point is on-course consequence).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{dispersion,course_management}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Iain Highfield, KOHR Golf Academy (SportsEdTV)$drill$, 'https://sportsedtv.com/blog/how-to-take-your-range-game-to-the-golf-course', 'oga', true),

($drill$Worst-Ball$drill$,
 $drill$Play two balls from every spot and always continue with the worse one — golf's most brutal honesty test, the game Tiger uses to expose and fix the quality of his bad shots.$drill$,
 90, 'off_tee', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Jack Backhouse (National Club Golfer) frames the lesson exactly: "golfers of all abilities can hit good shots close — the difference between the best players and the worst is the quality of their *worst* shots." Worst-ball punishes your misses instead of rewarding your bests, so it trains the skill that actually lowers scores: turning a would-be double into a bogey. You play two balls from each position and always advance the *worse* of the two, all the way into the hole. Errors compound — one bad ball forces you to hit the next shot from a worse spot — so a round balloons well above your handicap and every loose swing is felt. Tiger Woods is the game's famous practitioner; he reportedly shot a worst-ball 66 at his home course (Golf Digest, Tim Rosaforte) against a normal course record of 62 — a useful illustration of how hard par-quality worst-ball golf is. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players working on consistency and course management; it's deliberately punishing, so it's wrong for beginners and casual players (their scores spiral and the lesson drowns). **Setup.** A quiet course, your bag, two balls played from every position. Decide your length up front — Backhouse runs a 3-hole version when time is short; the full game is 9 or 18. **How.** From the tee hit two drives; play your next shot from the *worse* lie and again hit two; keep advancing the worse ball to the green. On the green, putt from the farther ball — Backhouse's rule is to finish each hole only on two consecutive made putts. Record your worst-ball score and chase it down over repeats. **Time & frequency.** A round (or 3 holes when pressed); not every session — it's an honesty audit, not a confidence builder. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete 9 worst-ball holes and beat the total over time. • Competitive: keep worst-ball within a handful of strokes of your normal score — the gap is your inconsistency. **Common mistakes.** Playing the better ball when you're tempted (defeats the entire point); skipping the two-putt finish; trying it on a busy course; expecting a good number (a bloated score is the design, not a failure).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{course_management,dispersion}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Jack Backhouse, PGA (National Club Golfer); popularized by Tiger Woods (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.nationalclubgolfer.com/golf-tips/long-game/on-course-scoring-drills-hole-worst-ball-challenge/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Better-Ball (Solo)$drill$,
 $drill$Play two balls from every spot and take the better one — a solo scramble that lets you feel what a genuinely good round feels like and shows you what your second swing fixes.$drill$,
 60, 'off_tee', '{course}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Jack Backhouse's better-ball is the optimistic mirror of worst-ball, and the confidence it builds is the point. You play two balls from each position and continue with the *better* of the two, all the way to the hole, so you get a second chance at every shot and you score well below your normal number. That does two things: it lets you experience — and start to expect — pars and birdies (most amateurs never feel a good round, so they never learn to stay in one), and it reveals your swing tendencies in real time. If your first ball leaks right, the second ball is a live chance to feel the fix (close the face, commit to the start line) rather than stew over the miss. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players who get down on themselves or play tentatively; anyone rebuilding confidence after a swing change or a bad stretch. **Setup.** A quiet course, your bag, two balls per position. Backhouse runs it over 3 holes; extend to 9 if the course is open. **How.** From the tee hit two drives; play your next shot from the *better* lie and again hit two; keep advancing the better ball into the hole. Note which ball you keep and why — the pattern (e.g. "the second drive is always straighter once I commit") is the takeaway. Track your better-ball score to see your ceiling. **Time & frequency.** A few holes to 9; great after a rough round to reset the feel of scoring. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete the loop and post a number well under your norm. • Developing/competitive: identify the consistent tendency your second ball corrects and carry that feel into a normal round. **Common mistakes.** Mindlessly taking the better ball without noting why (you lose the diagnostic value); playing it on a busy course; using it as your only practice (pair it with worst-ball, which trains the opposite weakness); not carrying the "expect good shots" mindset into real rounds.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{course_management,dispersion}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Jack Backhouse, PGA (National Club Golfer)$drill$, 'https://www.nationalclubgolfer.com/golf-tips/long-game/on-course-scoring-drills-3-hole-better-ball-challenge/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Mid-Iron Challenge 130/150/170$drill$,
 $drill$Turn nine quiet holes into a string of mini par-3s — drop a ball at 130, 150, and 170 yards on every green and score each like its own hole — for high-rep approach practice in real conditions.$drill$,
 90, 'approach', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw, PhD/PGA (Golf Insider), designed this to add the one thing a normal round can't — approach-shot *volume* — without losing the on-course context a range can't give (a real green, a real lie, real consequence, wind). On each hole you drop a ball at three fixed distances and play each as its own little par-3, so nine holes become a 27-shot approach test from your scoring yardages instead of the handful of approaches a normal round serves up. Fixed distances also make it a clean benchmark: 150 yards plays the same on hole 3 as hole 12, so you can see real improvement. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose iron play is the score-killer; it assumes a strike reliable enough that 130–170-yard targets are meaningful, so it's not a beginner drill. **Setup.** A quiet course (so you're not holding anyone up), your irons, a few balls, and a rangefinder or known yardages. **How.** On each of 9 holes, drop a ball at 130, 150, and 170 yards from that green; play each shot and hole out, treating each distance as a separate par-3. Score it: 3 points for a "birdie" (hole it or get up-and-down for a 2 on the mini par-3), 1 point for a par, 0 for worse. Total across the 9 holes (max 27 mini-holes) and chase the number over repeats. **Time & frequency.** A 9-hole loop at a quiet time; weekly or biweekly as approach work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete all 27 and establish a points baseline; note your weakest of the three distances. • Competitive: push the points total up and close the gap on your worst distance. **Common mistakes.** Playing it on a busy course; only hitting the comfortable distance; not holing out (the up-and-down or putt is part of the score); skipping the scoring so there's nothing to beat.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,dispersion}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, PGA (Golf Insider)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-practice-games-long-game/', 'oga', true),

($drill$On-Course Multi-Ball Mid-Iron Reps$drill$,
 $drill$Play nine quiet holes and drop a few extra approach balls on every hole — the simplest way to multiply real on-course approach reps without rebuilding your range habits.$drill$,
 75, 'approach', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw, PhD/PGA (Golf Insider), makes the case that the course — not the range — is where skills actually transfer, but a normal round gives you only ~14 approach shots, far too few to improve. The fix is volume *in context*: go out at a quiet time and, on each hole, drop and hit a few extra approach shots from real positions before playing on. You keep everything the range strips away — the actual lie, the actual target, the slope, the nerves of a shot that "counts" — while turning 9 holes into ~30 approach reps. It's not a scored game; it's a deliberate volume protocol that closes the rep gap without you having to go grind a bucket. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who don't get enough approach reps in real conditions; players whose range irons don't show up on the course. **Setup.** A quiet course, your irons, a shag bag of extra balls, and the discipline to let faster groups through. **How.** Play your normal ball, then on each hole drop ~3 extra balls from key approach spots (your typical mid-iron yardages and the spots you actually find yourself in) and hit them to the green — without holding up play behind you. Pick up and move on. ~3 reps × 9 holes ≈ 30 approaches in one loop. **Time & frequency.** A 9-hole quiet loop, 1×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete the loop with ~30 in-context approach reps; note whether contact/strike holds up vs the range. • Competitive: use the extra reps to dial a specific distance or trajectory under real conditions. **Common mistakes.** Doing it when the course is busy (the cardinal sin); dropping to perfect flat lies instead of realistic ones; turning it into a careless rake-and-hit (each rep gets a target and intent); not picking up promptly to keep pace.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,contact}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, PGA (Golf Insider)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-practice-range-vs-course/', 'oga', true),

($drill$3-Club Challenge$drill$,
 $drill$Play a round with only three clubs and no swapping — being forced to manufacture every shot turns you into a shot-maker and exposes how much you lean on having the perfect club.$drill$,
 90, 'approach', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** As Fergus Bisset lays out for Golf Monthly, stripping your bag to three clubs forces creativity and distance control that a full set lets you avoid. You can't just pull "the right club" — you have to choke down, hit three-quarter and knockdown shots, open the face, and dial one club up and down across a 30-yard range. That manufacturing is exactly the on-course skill that separates players who can score on an off day from players who fall apart when their stock yardage doesn't fit. It also reveals dependency: many golfers discover their game is fine once they stop obsessing over exact gaps. The constraint makes a familiar course feel new and trains adaptability under real lies and targets. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who are mechanical/one-dimensional and want shot-making range; it needs enough ball-control to be productive rather than just frustrating, so it's not for beginners. **Setup.** A quiet (or normal, it doesn't slow play) course. Choose exactly three clubs up front and decide whether the putter is one of the three or a free bonus — Golf Monthly's purist version counts the putter as one; many play it as a freebie. Bisset suggests picking "course-blind" (a versatile set like a long iron/hybrid, a mid iron, and a wedge), not clubs tailored to one layout. **How.** Play your normal round with only those three clubs, no swaps. On every shot, pick which of the three and how to flight/throttle it to fit the yardage and lie. Keep your normal stroke score. **Time & frequency.** A full round or 9 holes; occasionally, for variety and shot-making. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: finish the round and notice how close your score lands to normal — usually closer than expected. • Competitive: beat a target score with the three clubs, and carry the manufactured shots (knockdowns, chokes) back into your full-bag rounds. **Common mistakes.** Picking three clubs tailored to one course; quietly swapping a club mid-round; only ever hitting full shots instead of manufacturing; treating it as a novelty rather than committing to score.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,trajectory,course_management}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Fergus Bisset (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/features/the-game/which-clubs-would-you-choose-for-a-three-club-challenge-196382', 'oga', true),

($drill$Missamall (Miss-Them-All)$drill$,
 $drill$Play a round where you deliberately miss every green until you're inside 50 yards — strip away the safety net and the whole course becomes live short-game and scrambling practice.$drill$,
 90, 'around_green', '{course}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tyler Ferrell (Golf Smart Academy) turns a whole round into greenside reps with one rule: "on every hole, your goal is to avoid the green until you are within 50 yards of the hole." Because a normal round gives you only a handful of up-and-downs, your scrambling barely gets trained where it matters — under real pressure, off real lies. Missamall forces dozens of live recovery shots (chips, pitches, bunker shots, and the putts after them) in genuine on-course conditions, so the part of the game that most separates scores gets the volume it never otherwise sees. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who leak strokes around the green; players whose practice-green chipping doesn't survive contact with a real lie. It's deliberately self-handicapping, so keep beginners off it (or limit them to the gentler 9-hole variant). **Setup.** A quiet course, wedges and putter, your normal ball. **How.** Play each hole normally from tee until you're inside 50 yards, where your aim is to *not* hit the green sooner — then play in normally, trying to get up and down in as few shots as possible. If you accidentally land on the green from outside 50 yards, move the ball off the putting surface and continue. Track your scrambling: how often you save par from inside 50, how often you leave a makeable putt. **Structured 9-hole variant (from the source):** deliberately miss into three fairway-side spots, three from the rough, and three from a bunker, to force variety in both the recovery and the kind of miss. **Time & frequency.** A 9- or 18-hole quiet loop; biweekly short-game work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: get up-and-down on a third of your attempts; clean contact from rough and sand. • Competitive: up-and-down on half or more, including short-sided and bunker lies. **Common mistakes.** Doing it on a busy course; bumping the ball to a comfortable lie instead of playing the miss; only chipping (the putt after is part of the save); ignoring the bunker spots because they're hard (that's where the strokes hide).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Tyler Ferrell (Golf Smart Academy)$drill$, 'https://www.golfsmartacademy.com/golf-tips/miss-them-all/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Bingo-Bango-Bongo$drill$,
 $drill$A three-point-per-hole group game — first on the green, closest once everyone's on, first in the hole — that rewards getting on early, proximity, and finishing rather than raw length.$drill$,
 90, 'around_green', '{course}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** As James Colgan explains for GOLF.com, Bingo-Bango-Bongo awards three points on every hole — **bingo** to the first player on the green, **bango** to the player closest to the pin once everyone is on, and **bongo** to the first to hole out — so it trains the scoring skills that matter regardless of how far you hit it. A short hitter who gets on first, lags it close, and finishes can clean up against a bomber who's wild, which is why it sharpens proximity, lag putting, and decisiveness rather than ego-driven distance. The strict order of play is the engine of the game and a real on-course discipline. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players in a group; especially players who let length thinking override smart, proximity-first course management. *(This is a GROUP game — it needs 3–4 players.)* **Setup.** A normal round with a group of 3–4, a way to track points (commonly a small stake like $1/point). Agree the rules first. **How.** On every hole, three points are live. Bingo: the first ball on the green. Bango: once all balls are on, the one closest to the hole. Bongo: the first ball holed. Play strict order — the player farthest from the hole always goes first, and no gimmes — or the points break; if a player goes out of turn to grab bingo or bongo, the point passes to the next player to earn it. Tally points across the round; most points wins. **Time & frequency.** A full round whenever you play in a group. **Success metric.** Win the points total; the real win is consistently earning bango (proximity) and bongo (finishing) rather than chasing distance. **Common mistakes.** Playing out of turn (breaks the whole game); allowing gimmes (kills bongo); fixating on bingo (first on) and ignoring that bango rewards a smart lay-up close; forgetting that a poor drive can still win the hole by getting close and finishing.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{course_management,distance_control}', 'on_course',
 $drill$James Colgan (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/lifestyle/how-to-play-bingo-bango-bongo/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Never-3-Putt Round Tracker$drill$,
 $drill$Track three-putts hole by hole for a whole round and chase your handicap benchmark — the single most useful putting stat, because it isolates the speed control that causes blow-up holes.$drill$,
 90, 'putting', '{course}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF.com (citing the Golf Science Lab) flags three-putt count as the putting stat most worth tracking on the course, because it isolates a specific, fixable weakness — poor speed control on the first putt — rather than the noise of total putts. Your only job on each green is to two-putt or better; tally every three-putt across the round and try to beat your benchmark next time. A three-putt is unambiguous feedback you can act on, and structuring the round around *avoiding* the big number (instead of chasing makes) is exactly how lower scores happen. The benchmarks make it concrete: Arccos data (75M+ shots) shows roughly 1.6 three-putts/round for a 1–5 handicap, ~2.3 for 6–10, and ~3.2 for 15+; Shot Scope's by-score figures are ~3.2 for 100s-shooters, ~2.3 for 90s, ~1.5 for 80s, and ~0.9 for 70s. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the target simply scales to your handicap; it's especially valuable for players whose scores are wrecked by three-putts they don't even track. **Setup.** Any round, plus a way to mark three-putts (scorecard corner, phone, or a counter). **How.** Play normally. On every green, the goal is two putts or fewer; each time you take three or more, make a mark. At the end, total your three-putts and compare to the benchmark for your level. Next round, try to beat it. Over time, note *where* the three-putts come from (long lag? downhill? specific distances) and feed that into practice. **Time & frequency.** Every round — it's a habit, not a one-off. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: get under ~3 per round, then under 2. • Developing: ~1.5 or fewer. • Competitive: ~1 or fewer per round. **Common mistakes.** Counting total putts instead of three-putts (total putts is muddied by GIR and chip-ins); not recording where the three-putts happen; chasing made putts and leaving aggressive first putts way past; forgetting to actually log it (the tracking is the drill).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,lag}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Golf Science Lab (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/5-golf-stats-tracking-become-better-play-smart/', 'oga', true),

($drill$First-Putt Inside-the-Circle$drill$,
 $drill$On every long first putt during a round, forget about making it — just leave it inside a three-foot circle and score it pass/fail, because first-putt distance control is what kills three-putts.$drill$,
 90, 'putting', '{course}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Shot Scope's data shows the lever for three-putt avoidance is first-putt finishing distance: about 83% of all three-putts start from a first putt outside ~30 feet, and the average handicap golfer's first putt is ~18.5 feet. This on-course drill turns that into a played habit — on every green where your first putt is long (roughly 16+ feet), your only goal is to leave the ball inside a three-foot radius of the hole (a "circle" you picture around the cup); you are explicitly *not* trying to make it. Mark each long first putt pass (inside the circle) or fail and total it across the round. Because makes from inside three feet are near-automatic even for high handicaps and collapse beyond it, leaving the first putt inside the circle is what converts likely three-putts into tap-in two-putts. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players who three-putt from distance; a true beginner is better served first by a practice-green lag ladder, then this. **Setup.** Any round; visualize a 3-foot circle around each hole; a way to tally pass/fail. **How.** On every green, if your first putt is ~16 feet or longer, commit only to speed — roll it to finish inside the 3-foot circle, not to hole it. Mark pass or fail. Count how many of your long first putts finished inside the circle out of how many you faced, and beat that ratio next round. Short first putts you simply try to make as normal. **Time & frequency.** Every round; pairs naturally with the three-putt tracker. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: half your long first putts inside the circle. • Developing: ~70%. • Competitive: most of them, with almost no three-putts as a result. **Common mistakes.** Trying to make long putts (chasing them is what blows speed and causes three-putts); only counting whether you holed it instead of circle pass/fail; ignoring uphill/downhill speed adjustments; not actually tracking it.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'on_course',
 $drill$Shot Scope$drill$, 'https://shotscope.com/blog/stats/analysing-putting/', 'oga', true),


($drill$Narrowing Fairway Ladder$drill$,
 $drill$Set a 50-yard fairway on the range and hit three drives in a row inside it to advance — then narrow to 30 yards, then to 10 — and you can't move on until you clear the current width.$drill$,
 20, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind) builds pressure from a single rule: you can only move to the next stage after hitting three balls in a row inside the current "fairway." A miss doesn't just score badly — it costs you the streak you were building, and the tightening width turns mild discomfort into Tour-grade precision. Each stage replicates a different course reality: 50 yards is a generous fairway, 30 yards is a tight one, 10 yards is a corridor that demands committed start-line work. Because progress is gated and a single bad swing keeps you stuck, the drill manufactures the consequence-on-this-shot pressure a normal range bucket erases. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike, especially those whose driver "works on the range" but leaks under pressure. Not for beginners — you can spend the whole session at stage one and learn nothing. **Setup.** Two range targets (alignment sticks, flags, or terrain features) set ~50 yards apart to mark the edges of your first fairway. Driver, full bucket, a routine you actually use on the course. **How.** Run your full pre-shot routine on every ball. Hit three drives in a row inside the 50-yard fairway — if you miss any, the count resets but you stay at this width. After 3-of-3, narrow the fairway to ~30 yards and repeat. After 3-of-3 there, narrow to ~10 yards. Completing all three stages = nine consecutive fairways. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on driver work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clear the 50-yard stage cleanly; chip away at 30. • Competitive: complete all three stages in a session. **Common mistakes.** Counting non-consecutive hits; widening the fairway when frustrated (kills the consequence); skipping the routine to rush; quitting before you've fought through a reset (the reset is where the lesson lives).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind)$drill$, 'https://golfstateofmind.com/5-pressure-practice-drills-for-golf/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Five-in-a-Row Fairway$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a fairway-width target on the range and hit five drives in a row inside it — any miss and the count goes back to zero. Pure consecutive-make pressure.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind) keeps the rule painfully simple: five consecutive fairways, and a single miss resets you to zero. The cumulative tension of a streak is exactly what builds up on the course as you walk to the next tee with a card going well — and most golfers crack under it because they never rehearse it on the range. By stripping out laddering, scoring, and time and just demanding five-in-a-row from a fixed width, the drill makes every shot feel like the one that breaks the streak. The single-miss reset is the consequence; the only way out is committing to the start line you actually want, every single rep. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players already past basic strike who freeze on important tee shots. Skip it for beginners — the streak may never start and the lesson disappears in noise. **Setup.** Two range targets ~30 yards apart marking a single fairway width (configurable: wider for developing, tighter for competitive). Driver, full bucket. **How.** Full routine on every ball. Count consecutive drives that finish inside the fairway. Miss = back to zero. Stop only when you've strung five in a row. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly when working tee-shot commitment. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: reach five-in-a-row at a 40–50-yard width. • Competitive: five-in-a-row at a 25–30-yard width. **Common mistakes.** Counting half-misses ("close enough"); cheating the width when the reset bites; rushing reps after a streak starts (commit to the routine — the streak punishes rushing); doing it cold without a warm-up (the drill is the test, not the warm-up).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$David MacKenzie (Golf State of Mind)$drill$, 'https://golfstateofmind.com/driving-range-practice-create-pressure-while-you-practice/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Fairway Hit Challenge (7-of-10)$drill$,
 $drill$Hit seven of ten drives into your imaginary fairway before you're allowed to end the session — a Tour-grade standard with no shortcuts, reset the set if you fall short.$drill$,
 25, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Eric J. MacDonald, PGA, frames the pressure exactly: "hit 7 out of 10 drives in the fairway before finishing your range session." The mechanism isn't a score you track — it's a session lock, the same kind of "you don't leave until you clear the bar" standard Tour players use. 70% fairways with a driver on the range is roughly Tour-grade, so failing the bar is the default state, and the *only* way to leave is to actually find seven. The consequence — restart the set, no shortcut — produces real, escalating pressure as you get to ball 8, 9, 10 with the count tight. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who want a Tour-honest standard for driving accuracy; players who tell themselves they "drive it well" without ever testing it under a hard threshold. Not for beginners (the bar is unreachable). **Setup.** Range, driver, a defined imaginary fairway width (most players set 30–40 yards; tighter is harder), and the discipline to not bail on the rule. **How.** Hit 10 drives, count fairways. If you don't get to 7, you don't end the session — reset and hit another 10. Keep going until you clear it. Run your full routine on every ball — the routine is part of the test. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes (longer if you don't clear it quickly), 1×/week as a driver session finisher. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clear 7/10 at a generous 40-yard width within two sets. • Competitive: clear 7/10 at a 30-yard width within one set. **Common mistakes.** Quietly widening the fairway when stuck; bailing on the lock-in rule (no consequence, no pressure); only counting fairways without running the routine; doing it tired at the end of a bucket (decide up front this is the session).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Eric J. MacDonald, PGA$drill$, 'https://www.ericjmacdonaldpga.com/post/practice-under-pressure-how-to-simulate-on-course-stress-in-training', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pressure-Cooker (3-Target Sequence)$drill$,
 $drill$Pick three irons and three different targets and hit one shot per club, in order — miss any one and the whole three-shot sequence restarts from the first club.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Clickit Golf's resident PGA Pro frames the pressure exactly: "if you miss a target, you have to start the sequence over." The consequence isn't a worse score — it's losing every rep you just earned. Pair that with the source's mental hook ("imagine it is the 18th green" with a career-best on the line and run your full routine) and the drill turns a small set of approach shots into the back-nine pressure most players never rehearse. The sequence forces a club change every shot (so it's also random practice in flavor), but the consequence rule is the heart of the drill: a single missed window means you didn't really execute the previous two either. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who hit pure shots on the range but unravel when a single shot has to land. Not for beginners — the loop never closes and frustration is the only outcome. **Setup.** Range with three distinct target areas at different distances (a short-iron green, a mid-iron green, a wedge yardage), three clubs to match. Define the success window for each (treat a green-sized landing band as the bar). **How.** Choose your three clubs and three targets. Shot 1 with club A to target A; shot 2 with club B to target B; shot 3 with club C to target C. Full routine on every shot. Hit the success window on all three back-to-back to complete the drill. A miss at any shot → restart from shot 1, no exceptions. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on approach work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete the sequence within a generous window over a session. • Competitive: complete it cleanly at tighter windows. **Common mistakes.** Loosening the success window when stuck; skipping the routine to "just hit"; picking three similar clubs (kills the random element); quitting after one full reset (the second attempt is where the pressure starts compounding).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,contact,dispersion}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Clickit Golf (PGA Pro)$drill$, 'https://clickitgolf.com/master-your-game-3-range-drills-you-can-do-in-just-20-minute/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pelz Short Game Test$drill$,
 $drill$Take Dave Pelz's 80-shot short-game test across eight stations and score yourself against Tour and handicap thresholds — anything outside six feet earns zero, every weak shot is a wasted ball.$drill$,
 60, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dave Pelz (former NASA physicist turned short-game guru) built the canonical pass/fail short-game benchmark, and the consequence is built into the scoring: a chip outside 6 feet scores zero, between 3 and 6 feet scores 1 point, and inside 3 feet (including holed) scores 4 points. Because misses are worth nothing, every weak shot is a wasted ball — and your raw point total maps to a clear handicap-bracket bar you either clear or you don't. The 8 stations cover the full short-game test (pitches, sand, chips from fringe and rough, lob over bunker), so a single weakness gets caught. The Tour benchmarks make the test honest: you can't tell yourself you're a "good chipper" if you can't put up the number. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the thresholds self-calibrate to your handicap so the test is meaningful from beginner to scratch; especially useful for players who claim a strong short game without ever measuring it. **Setup.** A short-game area with chipping green, fringe, light rough, and a bunker; wedges and putter; 80 balls (or 10 you replay per station); a way to estimate 3-ft and 6-ft circles around the pin. **How.** Eight stations × 10 balls each: (1) 50-yd pitch, (2) 30-yd pitch, (3) 8-yd sand, (4) 15-yd long bunker blast, (5) 10-yd chip from light fringe, (6) 20-yd chip from light rough, (7) 15-yd short pitch, (8) 15-yd lob over a bunker. Score each shot: 0 outside 6 ft, 1 between 3–6 ft, 4 inside 3 ft (incl. hole-outs). Sum across all 80 shots. **Confirmed thresholds (per Pelz):** PGA Tour 120–150 · LPGA Tour 103–120 · 1–3 hcp 100–107 · 4–9 hcp 80–97 · 10–15 hcp 60–77 · 16–20 hcp 50–58 · 21–24 hcp 42–48 · 26–30 hcp 30–38 · <30 needs work. **Time & frequency.** ~60 minutes; monthly as a benchmark, not a daily drill. **Success metric, by level.** Clear the threshold band for your handicap. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the bunker stations (where the strokes hide); estimating distances generously; doing it in batches of one station for a week (the test is the full 80 in one session); comparing your raw score to a different distance protocol.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control,bunker}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dave Pelz (Pelz Golf, via Dog Leg News)$drill$, 'https://doglegnews.com/short-game-handicap-heres-the-test-to-determine-yours/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Last-Ball Chip-Out$drill$,
 $drill$Bring three (or six) balls; you must HOLE a chip before you retire that ball and move to a new location — the longer you go, the fewer chances you have left.$drill$,
 30, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Christy Longfield, PGA (GOLF Magazine Teacher to Watch), runs this drill — the same retire-on-make game Phil Mickelson uses in his own short-game practice. The pressure mechanism is consequence-by-attrition: you bring a fixed number of balls and you can only "retire" a ball by holing a chip with it. Move to a new location with the remaining balls, hole one out, retire it, move on. As balls get retired, you have fewer chances per spot until you're down to one ball and one shot at every new lie — which is exactly where pressure peaks. Longfield: "The longer you go, the harder it gets." Mickelson's version makes it even tighter (a missed 3-footer or a missed tight circle starts you over), so the lesson is consistent: misses bite. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose chips look fine but aren't actually holing out under pressure; players preparing for tournament short-game requirements. **Setup.** A short-game green with multiple lies and angles, wedge(s), 3 balls for a 30-min session or 6 for a 60-min session. **How.** From your first location, chip until one ball is holed; retire that ball. Move to a new location with the remaining balls (new lie, new distance, ideally varied trajectory); chip until one is holed; retire it. Repeat. The drill ends when you've retired every ball before time expires (or when you give up — both are honest signals). **Time & frequency.** 30 minutes (3 balls) or 60 minutes (6 balls), 1×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: retire all 3 balls within 30 minutes. • Competitive: retire 6 within 60 minutes, including from short-sided and tight lies. **Common mistakes.** Repeating the same easy spot (defeats the variety); skipping to a new location before holing out; using only one club when the lie demands a different shot; not actually setting a time cap (the time pressure is part of the consequence).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Christy Longfield, PGA (Golf.com); mirrors Phil Mickelson's practice$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/intense-practice-game-under-pressure-play-smart/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Pressure Up-and-Down (5-in-a-Row, 3-Miss Restart)$drill$,
 $drill$Get five up-and-downs in a row from varied lies — but rack up three total misses anywhere in the session and the whole thing restarts from zero.$drill$,
 25, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dr. Paul McCarthy (CPsychol, inaugural resident sport psychologist at St Andrews) layers two pressure mechanisms into one drill. First, "in a row" — any single missed up-and-down breaks your streak, so the count restarts to zero on each miss. Second, "three misses total" — accumulate three failures anywhere in the session and you start the whole drill over from scratch. Together they create a tight window: you can survive a streak break, but only a few of them. The drill rehearses exactly the cumulative pressure that turns a 75 into an 80 on the course — one save under pressure is one thing; five in a row with a counter against you is another. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose short game holds up in practice but cracks late in rounds; players preparing for tournaments. Beginners may never see a streak start — keep them off it. **Setup.** A short-game green with varied lies (fringe, light rough, slope, short-sided), a wedge and putter, a way to track streak count and total miss count. **How.** From a chosen lie, play a chip and one putt — making the up-and-down (chip + one-putt or chip-in) counts as a success and adds to your streak. Each missed up-and-down resets the streak to zero AND adds one to your total miss count. Hit 5 successes in a row to complete the drill. If your total miss count reaches 3 at any point, the drill ends and you restart from zero. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, 1–2×/week on scoring work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete one full 5-in-a-row run. • Competitive: complete it from genuinely varied/short-sided lies. **Common mistakes.** Choosing similar easy lies (kills the variety); conceding the comeback putt (the putt is half the test); abandoning the 3-miss rule when frustrated (the cap is the point); not tracking miss count.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dr. Paul McCarthy, CPsychol$drill$, 'https://www.drpaulmccarthy.com/post/ten-competitive-pressure-chipping-drills', 'oga', true),

($drill$Elimination Chipping (5-Station Survival)$drill$,
 $drill$Five chipping stations of varied lie and distance — land each chip within six feet of the pin to survive and advance; fail at any station and you're eliminated back to station one.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Dr. Paul McCarthy (CPsychol) frames it as a survival format: "5 chip shots from different locations. Must get within 6 feet on each to 'survive.'" Six feet is tight (tighter than the standard up-and-down threshold), and "elimination" means a single fail at any station sends you back to station one. The pressure compounds as you walk to the fifth station with a clean run going — exactly the feel of a late-round par save. By moving through varied lies and distances, the drill ensures you can't lean on one comfortable shape; every station is a different test, and the gate is the same. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players sharpening short-game precision under pressure; players who notice their chipping tightens up over the last few holes. **Setup.** A short-game green; 5 distinct stations placed around it with varied lie (fringe, rough, sand-side, slope, short-sided) and distance (e.g. 5, 10, 15, 20, 25 yards); a wedge and putter; the ability to estimate a 6-ft radius from each pin. **How.** Walk through the stations in order. At each, play one chip. Land it inside 6 feet of the pin → survive and move to the next station. Land outside 6 feet → eliminated; return to station 1 and start over. The drill is complete when you survive all 5 stations in a single attempt. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, weekly on short-game scoring. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: survive all 5 within a session. • Competitive: survive cleanly from tougher stations (short-sided, bunker-adjacent). **Common mistakes.** Setting all stations at similar distances (lose the variety); fudging the 6-ft gate when frustrated; only chipping (the drill is the chip — but the comeback putt is the next drill); skipping the hardest station because you can't get past it (that's where the work is).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Dr. Paul McCarthy, CPsychol$drill$, 'https://www.drpaulmccarthy.com/post/ten-competitive-pressure-chipping-drills', 'oga', true),

($drill$Golden Eight (Walters)$drill$,
 $drill$Hole three putts in a row from 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet — fifteen makes to complete the ladder, and a single miss anywhere drops you back to the start at two feet.$drill$,
 25, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach Ged Walters built this around the "Golden Distance" — the PGA Tour's ~87% make-rate inside 10 feet, the zone where good putters separate themselves from average ones. The drill stations are 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet, and at each you must hole three putts in a row before advancing. Walters' rule is unambiguous: "should you miss any putt, start the drill again from two feet." The consequence is total — fifteen makes are required to complete the ladder, and a single miss anywhere wipes everything. Pressure builds painfully as you reach the 8-footer and 10-footer with all progress on the line, which is the entire point: real-round putting is *playing for what you've already built*, not making fresh putts in isolation. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who want a Tour-honest short-putt standard and aren't broken by the restart rule. Not for beginners or casual players — the loop may not close and frustration is the only output. **Setup.** A flat practice green with a hole, tees at 2, 4, 6, 8, and 10 feet from the cup, a putter, a few balls. **How.** Start at 2 feet. Hole three in a row → advance to 4 feet. Hole three in a row → advance to 6 feet. Keep going through 8 and 10. Any single miss anywhere → restart from 2 feet, no exceptions. The drill ends when you make all 15 (three at each of the five stations) in succession. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes, 1×/week on short-putt pressure work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: complete the ladder within a session, even if it takes several attempts. • Competitive: complete it cleanly; consider adding a 12-ft station once the standard ladder is automatic. **Common mistakes.** Cheating the distances; counting non-consecutive makes; abandoning the restart rule when the 10-footer breaks the streak (the breakdown is exactly the rehearsal you need); rushing the short ones because they "should" go in.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Ged Walters, Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/the-average-pga-tour-golfer-makes-87-of-putts-from-inside-10-feet-this-expert-drill-can-help-you-improve-your-success-from-the-golden-distance', 'oga', true),

($drill$Faxon Compass Pressure Drill$drill$,
 $drill$Hit twelve putts from 4, 6, and 8 feet around all four sides of the hole, scoring par on the short ones and birdie on the eights — Tour standard is two-under across the twelve.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** 8-time PGA Tour winner Brad Faxon designed this as a pressure test, not a scoring game — "Can you shoot under par? If you miss one of the shorter shots, can you overcome any anxiety to make an additional eight-footer?" Three balls at each of three distances (4, 6, 8 ft) at each of four compass points around the hole = 12 putts. The 4-footers and 6-footers are "pars" (you must hole them to make par); the 8-footers are "birdies" (a make scores -1). Tour standard is 2-under across the 12 (10 of 12 made); a recreational benchmark is anything under par. The pressure isn't a tracked score you beat next time — it's the threshold itself, plus the fact that a single miss inside 6 feet immediately puts every remaining 8-footer under pressure to claw back to par. The 4-direction layout exposes any directional weakness in your stroke (left-to-right break vs right-to-left). **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with sound mechanics testing themselves; especially players who need to lock in 6-footers under tournament pressure. **Setup.** A flat practice green with a hole; tees at 4, 6, and 8 feet from the cup at four cardinal points (N/S/E/W) around it (12 tees total); a putter; 3 balls. **How.** Walk one cardinal at a time. At the N tee: hit the 4-footer (par if holed), then the 6-footer (par), then the 8-footer (birdie if holed). Move to S, then E, then W. After all 12 putts, total your score relative to par-12. Tour standard: 2-under (≥10 of 12 made). Recreational target: under par. Beat the threshold to "pass." **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: at or under par across the 12. • Competitive: 2-under or better, holing all the inside-6-ft putts. **Common mistakes.** Setting the tees at different distances around the hole (defeats the symmetry); skipping the long ones; not running a routine on every putt (the routine IS the drill at this distance); chasing a personal-best total instead of the pass/fail standard (that turns it into skill_game).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Brad Faxon (Golf.com / Nick Dimengo)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/brad-faxon-pressure-putting-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Putting Ladder (Nicol & Morris)$drill$,
 $drill$Set a semicircle of tees a grip-length behind the hole as a safe zone, then putt from progressively longer stations — short or past the zone and you reset to the first station.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Gary Nicol (PGA) and Karl Morris (renowned mental coach), authors of *The Lost Art of Putting*, built a lag-distance pressure drill that's harsher than it sounds. A semicircle of tees roughly one grip-length (~1 ft) behind the cup defines a "safe zone" between the cup and the back tees. From each station, a putt that holes out OR finishes inside that safe zone counts as success and advances you to the next station. A putt that comes up short of the cup, or rolls past the back tees, resets you to station one. The drill rewards the exact thing tour pros do — die the ball inside a small zone past the cup — and punishes both timid and aggressive lag putts equally. Because the zone is tight, every long station becomes a real speed test under restart pressure. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose lag putting leaks strokes; especially players who three-putt either because they leave it short OR blow it past. The safe-zone forgiveness makes it less brutal than the Golden Eight, but the restart still bites. **Setup.** A practice green with room for stations from ~3 to ~15 feet, tees pushed into the green about a grip-length behind a chosen hole forming a semicircle (the safe zone), a putter, a few balls. Set 4–5 stations at increasing distances (e.g. 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 ft). **How.** Start at station 1. Putt one ball: hole it OR stop it inside the safe zone behind the cup → advance to station 2. Short of the cup, or past the back tees → restart at station 1. Continue until you complete the longest station. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1–2×/week on lag work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete the ladder within a session. • Developing: complete it cleanly without resetting on the long station. • Competitive: extend to a 20-ft top station. **Common mistakes.** Making the safe zone too generous; ignoring the "short of the cup" half of the rule (short is the bigger lag fault); cheating the station distances; quitting after one restart at the long station (that's where the drill earns its keep).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'pressure_game',
 $drill$Gary Nicol (PGA) & Karl Morris, The Lost Art of Putting$drill$, 'https://www.thelostartofgolf.com/458/', 'oga', true),


($drill$Five-Ball Driver Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Tee five drivers and ladder the speed — an easy 100-yarder first, then 125, 150, 175, and a controlled rip — so you arrive on the first tee with the right driver tempo without burning your back.$drill$,
 5, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Scott Munroe built this as the *last* step of the warm-up — the bridge between the range and the first tee. Most amateurs walk to the first hole having either grinded the driver in their final ten range balls (tight, tired, swinging at 100%) or never touched it (cold). Munroe's fix is a graduated five-swing speed ramp: ball position off the lead big toe to encourage the slight upswing catch a driver wants, then build speed across five balls — slow at first to find posture, faster each ball, finishing with one "kill it" swing where you rip without losing balance or posture. The point isn't to grind anything — it's to teach the body that adding speed doesn't mean adding tension. On the first tee you then choose a tempo between balls 4 and 5 (the controlled-fast version), not the max swing. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose driver feels different on the course than the range; especially players who arrive at the first tee tight. **Setup.** Tee, driver, 5 balls. **How.** Tee five balls with ball position off the lead big toe. Ball 1 = slow full swing, ~100-yard carry, all focus on posture and finish position. Ball 2 = ~125 yards. Ball 3 = ~150. Ball 4 = ~175. Ball 5 = "kill it" — rip as hard and as far as you can without losing your spikes or your finish. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes — the final block of a warm-up, every pre-round. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: clean contact on 4 of 5, finish balanced. • Developing: ball-flight shape consistent. • Competitive: ball 5's shape matches ball 4's — speed without losing the swing. **Common mistakes.** Adding power instead of speed (causes tension); skipping ball 1 (the slow rep teaches posture); ripping ball 5 with terrible balance (the cap is balance, not yardage); doing it before the rest of the warmup (it's the finisher, not the opener).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Scott Munroe, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/golf-tips-warm-up-your-driver-with-just-5-swings/', 'oga', true),

($drill$First-Hole Dress Rehearsal$drill$,
 $drill$Finish your range warm-up by playing the first hole on the range — your actual tee shot to your actual target, then the approach club you expect — to transfer focus from mechanics to the shot that matters.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teachers Kevin Weeks and Brady Riggs both teach the same closing move on a warm-up: stop hitting "range balls" and start playing your actual scorecard. Weeks's instruction is direct — "play the first hole or two on the range, executing the tee shot they want and then the club they expect to hit into the green." Riggs adds the first par-3's tee shot. The mechanism is transfer: your last few balls happen WITH the same target, pre-shot routine, and shot shape you'll execute on the first tee, so the focus shifts from swing mechanics to "where is this going." It also handles first-tee nerves by rehearsing the literal shot before it counts. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players, especially anyone who tightens up on the first tee or arrives at the course thinking about mechanics instead of targets. **Setup.** Range with a view of the kind of fairway you'll face on hole 1; the actual driver / 3-wood / iron you'll hit off the first tee plus the approach club you typically need. **How.** After the regular tempo and iron build, hit the first-tee club with your full pre-shot routine to a target representing the fairway you'll need. Then hit the approach club you expect into hole 1's green. If your course starts with a par-3 or has an early par-3, hit that tee shot too. 2–3 specific shots, full routine, no rakes. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes — the closing block of every pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hit the tee-shot target reasonably. • Developing: execute both shots to a real target with your routine. • Competitive: shot shape matches your intent on both. **Common mistakes.** Treating it as just more reps (skip the routine and the transfer is lost); hitting clubs you won't use; not visualizing the actual hole; doing it cold (it's the closer, not the opener).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{course_management,dispersion}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Kevin Weeks & Brady Riggs, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/golf-how-to-warm-up-how-many-range-balls/', 'oga', true),

($drill$7-Iron Calibration Warm-Up$drill$,
 $drill$Start with the 7-iron — not so short you skip the body warming, not so long you swing tight — and at 70% effort find your low point and calibrate face direction before reaching for anything longer.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher John Dunigan argues the 7-iron is the right warm-up club for adults: a wedge is so short it skips the body-warming benefit, and the driver creates tension before you've moved. The 7-iron sits in the sweet spot — long enough to wake the body up, short enough to find center contact without forcing. Dunigan's protocol starts with a few baseball-style rotational swings (one lefty, one righty) to loosen turn, then 7-irons at 70% effort with one focus: locate the low point of the swing so contact is ball-first, turf-after. Once strikes are clean, he calibrates face direction without changing setup — hit one shot intentionally left of target, one right, one straight, manipulating only the grip and face through the early downswing. Only then does he move to the longer clubs "using a similar process." **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic contact who want a structured on-ramp; beginners should still start with a wedge ladder. **Setup.** Range, 7-iron, no aids. **How.** Baseball swings — a couple lefty, a couple righty — to loosen rotation. Then 7-irons at ~70% effort, focus only on low point (ball, then turf). Once strikes are clean, calibrate face: 1 shot intentionally left, 1 right, 1 straight, varying only grip/face direction. After ~15–20 minutes of 7-iron work, move to the longer clubs with the same easy-effort, low-point-first approach. **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes; every pre-practice or pre-round warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean ball-then-turf contact on most 7-irons; recognize left/right shape difference. • Competitive: all three calibration shapes go where you call them. **Common mistakes.** Going to 100% on the first ball; starting with a wedge or driver instead of the 7-iron (loses the calibration value); skipping the baseball swings; rushing past the calibration shapes to "real" shots.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$John Dunigan, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/7-iron-ideal-warm-up-club-john-dunigan/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Spieth Wedge Build-Up (20→40→Full)$drill$,
 $drill$Feet together with the 60° lob, rock the ball 20 yards, then 40, then full — Spieth's wedge primer that extends swing length, not effort, before walking through the rest of the bag.$drill$,
 10, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Jordan Spieth's pre-round wedge build-up — codified by long-time coach Cameron McCormick — is the cleanest published example of "extend length, not effort." Standing feet-together with a 60° lob wedge forces a balanced, body-driven swing (no leg drive to override tempo), and you start by rocking the club back and forth with only enough force to fly the ball about 20 yards. Each subsequent ball flies a few yards farther than the last as swing length, not effort, gradually extends. Over about 7 minutes the wedge stretches from ~20 yards to ~40 yards to a full lob-wedge swing, at which point Spieth progresses up through short irons → mid-irons → long irons → hybrids → woods → driver, picking a real target on every shot and stretching the gap between balls to simulate on-course pace. The whole sequence trains tempo through length, which is exactly the tempo you want on the course. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who lose tempo when they switch clubs cold; players who want a tour-tested pre-round routine. **Setup.** Range, 60° lob wedge to start (or your highest-lofted wedge), then the rest of the bag in order. **How.** Feet together, 60° wedge, ball forward of center. Rock the club back and through with just enough swing length to fly the ball ~20 yards. Each ball, extend the swing length a little, building toward ~40 yards, then full wedge swings. Then progress through clubs: short irons (small handful), mid-irons, long irons / hybrids, woods, finishing with 4–5 drivers. Real target on every shot; stretch the time between balls toward the end to mimic on-course pace. **Time & frequency.** 10–12 minutes for the wedge build-up alone (~7 min wedge, then ~5 min progression); part of a longer warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: wedge yardages step up cleanly without effort changes. • Competitive: smooth tempo carries from the lob wedge through the driver. **Common mistakes.** Adding effort instead of length (kills the point); skipping feet-together for "real" stance early (loses the balance constraint); rushing through balls to get to the driver; finishing with too many drivers (Spieth ends on 4–5, not 20).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Jordan Spieth / Cameron McCormick (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/news/tournaments/jordan-spieth-complete-warmup-minute-by-minute/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Half-to-Full Iron Tempo Ramp$drill$,
 $drill$Stage your iron warm-up by swing length — five or six wedges from hip-high to shoulder-high to full to three-quarter — then move to the mid-iron at the on-course tempo you just found.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Tour player Mark Baldwin (via GOLFTEC) and XXIO golf pro Nathalie Sheehan (golf.com) describe the same canonical pre-round iron sequence: 5–6 wedges progressing by swing length — hip-high → shoulder-high → full → back down to three-quarter — so you finish the wedge block in the swing length you'll actually use on the course. Then 5–6 shots with a 6- or 7-iron, then a few with the longer clubs you'll hit off the first tee. The hip-shoulder-full-three-quarter staging is the key insight: most amateurs jump from half-swings to full and then end on full, but the three-quarter finish is what you actually deliver under pressure on a real shot. Sheehan's framing: it's not technique work, it's "a feel for the day." Baldwin's framing: it's enough reps to find center contact without grinding. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Most universally applicable iron warm-up in the corpus — scales by club choice (any wedge + any mid-iron + a couple of fairway clubs). **Setup.** Range, a wedge, a mid-iron (6 or 7), and the longer clubs you'll hit off the first tee. **How.** Wedges first — 5–6 shots, swing-length sequence: 2 hip-high, 1–2 shoulder-high, 1–2 full, 1 three-quarter finishing shot. Then 5–6 shots with a 6- or 7-iron at the tempo you found. Then a few shots with the longer clubs (hybrid, fairway wood, or driver) — small reps, just to wake them up. Real target on every ball. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes; every pre-round or pre-practice warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean wedge contact at full; mid-iron lands somewhere useful. • Developing: wedge yardages step up by length; mid-iron flight matches what you'll use. • Competitive: the three-quarter wedge feels like an on-course shot. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the half-swings (the body-warming phase); ending on full instead of three-quarter (loses the on-course finish); hitting all the same club to "groove" it (this is warmup, not blocked practice); spending all your range balls on the driver.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Nathalie Sheehan (Golf.com); Mark Baldwin, PGA Tour (GOLFTEC)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/smart-way-maximize-warmup-live-well-play-well/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Daly Lead-Hand Chip Primer$drill$,
 $drill$Daly's pre-round chip primer — ten to twenty short chips with the lead arm only, fifty-two degree wedge — strips out trail-hand manipulation so the body and tempo are calibrated before the first real shot.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** John Daly's pre-round chip primer has lasted because it does one thing cleanly: it removes the trail hand's ability to flip, scoop, or grab through impact. With the lead arm only, you can't manipulate the clubface — you have to let the clubhead fall onto the ball and use the shoulders and chest to swing through. That's the exact tempo and feel you want from a chip when it actually counts. Daly's protocol is short and specific: a 52° wedge, narrow stance, ball centered with a slight forward shaft lean, and 10–20 small chips with the lead hand only before every round. The drill calibrates strike (the lead arm naturally bottoms out at the same place every time) and primes the body to feel a chip motion rather than a hand motion. **Who it helps most.** Every level — universal warm-up mechanic. Especially useful for players who flip or scoop chips under pressure. **Setup.** A short-game green, a 52° wedge (a 54/56/58/60 also works), 10–20 balls. **How.** Narrow stance, ball centered, slight forward shaft lean. Remove the trail hand from the grip. Swing back and through with the lead arm only, letting the clubhead fall onto the ball and holding the face square (no rollover) through impact. Short chips only — 10–20 balls. Then transition to normal two-handed chips with the same tempo and feel. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes; every pre-round and pre-short-game session. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most balls; no scoops. • Developing/competitive: tempo and strike pattern carry into the two-handed chips that follow. **Common mistakes.** Trying to swing too far (the lead-only motion is short — long swings break down); rolling the face through impact (the point is to keep it square); using a long iron (the wedge's loft is part of the drill); skipping the transition to two-handed chips at the end (the transfer is the goal).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,tempo}', 'warmup',
 $drill$John Daly (Wild Golf / Daily Duffer)$drill$, 'https://wildgolf.com/p/best-value-pnw-gold-mountain-golf-olympic-course-bremerton-wa-john-daly-one-handed-drill', 'oga', true),

($drill$Shaw Course-Sim Chip Opener$drill$,
 $drill$A short target-focused opener — five to ten chips from eight to ten yards off the green trying to hole each — that wakes up strike and aim before the round starts.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw, PhD/PGA (Golf Insider), built this as the opening block of a pre-round short-game warm-up. The mechanic is deliberately simple: pick one accessible hole on the practice green from 8–10 yards off, and hit 5–10 chips trying to hole each one. No varied lies, no varied trajectories, no clubs to rotate — one shot, one target, repeated. Shaw's coaching point is that "trying to hole each shot" is a target-focus device, not a scoring goal — the *attempt* to hole out is what tunes aim and strike, even when the ball doesn't fall. The single-shot repetition wakes up the strike quality and aim you need to be sharp on the course without sliding into pressure or scoring. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Strongest fit for beginners and casual players who need a simple, single-shot on-ramp; still useful as the opening block of a developing or competitive player's short-game warm-up. **Setup.** A short-game green, your standard chipping wedge, 5–10 balls, a hole 8–10 yards from a spot just off the green. **How.** Pick the hole and the spot. Hit 5–10 standard chips, trying to hole each one. Same lie, same target, same club — the point is target focus and strike, not variety. **Time & frequency.** 5–10 minutes as the opening block of every pre-round short-game warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most chips; ball finishes inside 10 ft. • Developing/competitive: most chips finish inside 5 ft; the occasional one falls. **Common mistakes.** Trying to make this a scoring drill (it isn't — the "try to hole each" is a focus device); varying lies or clubs (loses the simplicity); skipping it because it feels too easy (the simplicity is the point — it's the on-ramp); doing it as the only short-game work.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, PGA (Golf Insider)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-warm-up-routine/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Speed Station (15-Inches-Past Lag Primer)$drill$,
 $drill$Stick two tees fifteen inches apart on the practice green, then putt five lag balls trying to leave each one between them — calibrate pace to today's greens without a hole to make or miss.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach Paul Foston designed this around Dave Pelz's "perfect speed for any putt is 15 inches past the hole" principle, applied as a hole-free pace primer. Two tees pushed into the green 15 inches apart create a narrow target zone; you putt 5 balls from a comfortable lag distance trying to leave each one *between* the two pegs. Removing the hole is the key insight — most amateurs roll a few lag putts at the cup before the round, see them lip out or finish 6 feet past, and walk to the first tee with putting anxiety. With no hole, there's no missed putt to negotiate. The narrow zone replaces binary made/missed feedback with graded calibration: between the tees = perfect pace for today's greens; 6 inches off = a small miss; 4 feet off = the speed errors that produce three-putts. Five putts is enough to find the day's pace, fast. **Who it helps most.** Every level. The most universally applicable pre-round putting warm-up — no hole, no break required, just speed. **Setup.** A practice green, 2 tees pushed in 15 inches apart somewhere on a flat section, a putter, 5 balls. Pick a comfortable lag distance (20–30 ft is typical). **How.** Stand at your chosen distance. Putt the 5 balls one at a time toward the tee gate, trying to leave each ball *between* the two pegs. Don't aim at a hole — aim at the zone. The feedback is finishing position relative to the tees: in between = perfect; 6 inches off = adjust slightly; 4+ feet off = today's greens are faster/slower than you thought. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; the opening block of every pre-round putting warm-up. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: 2 of 5 inside the tee gate. • Developing: 3 of 5. • Competitive: 4 of 5 with no outliers beyond a foot. **Common mistakes.** Aiming at a hole anyway (the point is to remove that feedback); putting from a distance where you can't reach the zone consistently; doing it on broken slope (a flat section is the right surface for pace calibration); skipping it because "I'll feel the speed during the round" (the first hole is too late).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{distance_control,lag}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Paul Foston, Golf Monthly Top 50 (citing Dave Pelz)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/forget-the-hole-when-putting-on-the-practice-green-try-this-essential-pre-round-drill-instead', 'oga', true),

($drill$Three-Ball Touch Cluster$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a general target on the green ten to fifteen feet away — no hole — and roll three balls trying to leave them touching each other. Tests stroke repeatability cleanly, no read or line distractions.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Monthly Top 25 Coach Andrew Reynolds's three-ball cluster is a stroke-repeatability primer that removes both the cup and any defined zone. You pick a vague general target on the practice green at 10–15 feet (not a hole), roll three balls one after the other, and try to leave all three finishing touching each other. With no hole and no zone, the only feedback signal is whether the three balls *cluster* — which isolates stroke repeatability and tempo independently of read or line. That's exactly what fails after a layoff or in the first 10 minutes of warm-up: stroke length and tempo drift, but you can't see it when you're focused on a hole. Three balls together = stroke is repeatable; three balls scattered = it's not, and you've got 4 sets left to find it. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players; beginners benefit less here (they need explicit target feedback to learn what good contact feels like). Especially useful for players who putt well in practice when the line is obvious but lose stroke quality on real greens. **Setup.** A practice green, 3 balls, a putter, a general target 10–15 feet away (a discolored patch, a divot, an edge — *not* a hole). **How.** Stand at your chosen distance from the general target. Roll the 3 balls one after another, full routine on each, aiming for the same vague target. Walk down and look at where they finished — try to leave all three close enough to touch. Repeat the three-ball set 4–5 times. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; pairs naturally after the Speed Station or as a standalone stroke primer. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: balls cluster within ~2 feet of each other. • Developing: cluster within ~6 inches. • Competitive: three balls genuinely touching. **Common mistakes.** Picking a hole anyway (the cluster is the feedback); aiming at three different targets (kills the variance signal); rushing the three balls (each gets a routine); skipping the walk-down to look at the cluster (the diagnosis is half the drill).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,distance_control}', 'warmup',
 $drill$Andrew Reynolds, Golf Monthly Top 25 (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/videos/putting-tips/golf-pre-round-putting-drills', 'oga', true),


($drill$Chair-Behind Hips$drill$,
 $drill$Set a chair behind your butt at address and feel both glutes press into it through the swing — if the connection breaks, you've early-extended, which is the root cause of most blocked drives and snap-hooks.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Ludvig Åberg's go-to drill, codified by Golf Digest #1-ranked teacher Mark Blackburn, attacks early extension — the hip-thrust toward the ball in the downswing that costs the room your arms need to deliver the club from the inside. When the hips thrust forward, the body has to make space somewhere, so the arms get stuck (block-right) or flip past (snap-hook) and impact feels armsy. Placing a chair (or couch arm) directly behind the butt at address gives a physical reference: the right glute presses into the chair on the backswing, and the left glute should press back into the chair through transition and impact. The moment butt-to-chair contact breaks, you've early-extended — instant, kinesthetic feedback the player can't argue with. **What it trains.** Posture retention; hip rotation rather than hip thrust. **When to use.** When drives are leaking right (stuck blocks) or snap-hooking; when impact feels armsy/flippy; or as a periodic check on swing posture. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who recognize the fault. Skip for beginners — they don't yet have the diagnosis. **Setup.** A chair, couch arm, or wall behind your butt at address. Driver, no net required — these can be rehearsal swings or hit balls if you have space and a low net. **How.** Address with the chair just touching your butt. Make rehearsal swings (or shots) feeling the right glute press into the chair on the backswing; through transition the left glute should press back into the chair, not pull away. Åberg's pre-shot feel: "pressure shifts from my right heel to my left toe" without the hips thrusting forward. 10–15 rehearsals, then 5–10 shots checking the connection. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly on body work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: maintain butt-chair contact on rehearsals and most shots. • Competitive: ball flight pattern shifts (no more stuck blocks/snap-hooks). **Common mistakes.** Cheating the chair gap; only doing rehearsals (transfer needs reps with a ball); confusing this with a posture drill for the upper body (the hips are the target); rushing the swing.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Ludvig Åberg / Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/ludvig-aberg-go-to-swing-drill-fix-early-extension-get-more-shaft-lean', 'oga', true),

($drill$Grass-Skim Sweep$drill$,
 $drill$With the driver set just kissing the tops of the tee-height grass, make swings that only skim the blades — anything deeper means you're hitting down on a club that wants to be hit up.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Golf Digest Best Young Teacher Jason Guss strips driver attack-angle work down to one tactile cue. Most amateurs apply iron mechanics to a teed-up ball and hit slightly down on it — that adds dynamic loft, spikes spin, and kills carry. The fix isn't to "stop hitting down" abstractly; it's to feel a sweep. Setting the driver sole just kissing the tops of tee-height grass turns the grass into a physical reference: swings that only graze the blade tips are sweeping; swings that catch the blades deeper are still descending. Guss frames the feel as "you're sweeping it, and learning to hit up on it instead of hitting down on the ball." **What it trains.** Positive attack-angle grooved into muscle memory without a launch monitor. **When to use.** When drives launch low and spinny, when shots balloon or fall out short, or when face-tape shows contact on the top half (skyball pattern). **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players with the descending-strike driver pattern. Competitive players already have positive AoA from launch-monitor calibration. **Setup.** A backyard / tee-box patch of grass roughly tee-height, or a range mat with brushes. Driver, no ball. **How.** Address as you would a teed ball, but with the sole sitting *on top of* the grass blades. Make full swings whose only goal is to skim the very tops of the blades through the impact zone — no deeper. 15–20 rehearsals. When the feel is clear, transition to teed balls applying the same sweep. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1–2×/week when working trajectory. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: visibly higher launch and more carry on teed drives after a session. • Developing: ball flight matches the "high-launch low-spin" target driver number. **Common mistakes.** Setting the grass too short (no feedback); chopping at the blades to "feel" the swing (that's the wrong motion); skipping the transfer to actual balls; doing it on a mat without grass.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Jason Guss (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/this-is-the-easiest-backyard-driver-drill-weve-ever-seen', 'oga', true),

($drill$Belt-Loop Hip Turn$drill$,
 $drill$Slide an alignment stick through your front belt loops and rotate the backswing until the stick passes a second stick on the ground — forces a real hip turn, not a lateral slide, so the downswing isn't behind schedule.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher John Scott Rattan built a tactile fix for one of the most common amateur faults: sliding off the ball in the backswing instead of rotating. A slide leaves the upper body stacked over the trail leg with no real turn — the downswing then comes from the arms and is always behind schedule. Sliding an alignment stick through the lead-side belt loops gives the hips a visible reference. A second stick on the ground at about a 50° angle (away from target) is the gate. As you swing back, the belt-loop stick has to rotate past the ground stick — which it can only do if the hips genuinely turn behind the ball, recruiting the right hip rather than just the left knee. **What it trains.** Backswing rotation depth (hip turn vs lateral slide). **When to use.** When drives spray both ways inconsistently; when the chest stays over the ball at the top (no real turn); when the player feels like they "slide" off the ball. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players whose path varies wildly because the backswing turn is incomplete. **Setup.** Two alignment sticks. Push one through the lead-side belt loops so it protrudes toward the target. Lay the second on the ground between your feet at about a 50° angle away from the target line. Driver. **How.** Address. Swing back, rotating enough that the belt-loop stick visibly passes over the ground stick. Trail-foot big toe stays on the ground; head stays tall. Rehearse the turn 5–10 times without a ball, then hit shots while keeping the same rotation depth. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week when working backswing rotation. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: belt-loop stick clears the ground stick on most swings; ball flight tightens. • Competitive: full rotation locked in and reproducible. **Common mistakes.** Sliding the hips off the ball to clear the stick (the rotation is the point); collapsing the trail-foot heel up (lose ground connection); forgetting the head should stay tall and centered; doing it without a club for too long (transfer needs the swing).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$John Scott Rattan, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/alignment-stick-drill-ball-striking-john-scott-rattan/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Block-Behind-Club Push$drill$,
 $drill$Place a block or hardcover book behind the clubhead at address and push it back as you start the swing — primes ground pressure and load instead of the lift-up that costs most amateurs power.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** David Leadbetter, with biomechanist Jean-Jacques Rivet (Golf Digest), found that pros press about 125% of bodyweight INTO the ground at the takeaway while amateurs only push about 70% — most golfers actually lighten their ground force by lifting up as they start back, which kills coil and downstream power. The fix is a tactile primer: place a wood block or hardcover book on the ground directly behind the clubhead at address, and push the block backward (away from target) as you take the club away. You can't push the block by lifting up — only by pressing through the feet — so the drill grooves a grounded, loaded takeaway in seconds. **What it trains.** Ground pressure / loading the takeaway; coil. **When to use.** When drives feel weak or short despite full swings; when the player feels like they're lifting up out of posture in the takeaway. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike. **Setup.** A wood block, hardcover book, or sturdy object behind the clubhead at address. Driver or 6-iron. **How.** Address. Push the block backward as you start the backswing — feel pressure build under the right heel and the ball of the left foot from the extra effort. Don't lift the chest. Then complete a normal backswing maintaining that grounded feel. 10 rehearsals, then a set of 10 shots applying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1–2×/week on power/sequence work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: feel ground pressure increase on the takeaway. • Competitive: distance gains carry into normal swings without the block. **Common mistakes.** Lifting up to "push" the block (defeats the point); pushing only with the arms; skipping the transfer to normal swings; treating it as a once-and-done (it's a feel that erodes — revisit it).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$David Leadbetter / Jean-Jacques Rivet (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/david-leadbetter-power', 'oga', true),

($drill$Three-Shadow-Balls Center$drill$,
 $drill$With the sun at your back, line three balls through your shadow at belt, chest, and head — each part of your shadow should stay over its ball through the swing, exposing sway and reverse-pivots in real time.$drill$,
 15, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest #1-ranked teacher) uses your own shadow as a free swing-checker. With the sun at your back, address a ball so your shadow falls clearly in front of you. Place three balls in a straight line through the middle of your shadow: one at your belt buckle, one at the center of your chest, one at your forehead. Now swing. The goal is for each part of your shadow to stay over its corresponding ball — if the belt shadow drifts off the lower ball you swayed; if the head shadow rises off the upper ball you stood up; if the head shadow goes the wrong way you reverse-pivoted. The shadow is a continuous visual record of your turn quality, available the moment you step outside on a sunny day. **What it trains.** Staying centered over the ball through the whole swing (anti-sway + anti-reverse-pivot). **When to use.** When drives are fat/thin/off-the-heel from a swaying low point, or when the player feels off-balance at the top. **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players with sway or reverse-pivot patterns; competitive players use it as a periodic check. **Setup.** Sunny day, sun behind you, an open hitting area where your shadow falls clearly. Three balls placed through the middle of your shadow at belt, chest, and forehead levels. Any club (driver fine). **How.** Address with the shadow setup. Make swings — each part of the shadow should stay over its corresponding ball through backswing and downswing. The head may dip slightly below the top ball (that's normal); what you're avoiding is lateral drift. Self-correct, rehearse, then hit balls with the same centeredness. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes whenever the sun cooperates. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: identify which way you sway and stop it. • Developing: shadow holds its ground over all three balls on most swings; ball-strike pattern tightens. **Common mistakes.** Doing it under cloud cover (no shadow, no feedback); standing too far from the balls so the shadow doesn't clearly mark them; freezing to "hold" position instead of swinging naturally; ignoring head drift because it "looks small" (it isn't).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Mark Blackburn (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/Justin-Rose-golf-swing-drill-video-mark-blackburn', 'oga', true),

($drill$Driver Setup Primer$drill$,
 $drill$Tee the ball so two-thirds of it sits above the driver crown, play it forward at the lead toe, drop the trail shoulder slightly — the setup that lets the driver swing up, before the swing even starts.$drill$,
 10, 'off_tee', '{range}', '{beginner,casual,developing}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Tour pro Daniel Berger's pre-shot setup primer (Golf Digest) fixes the address position that quietly sabotages most amateur drivers: a low tee, a centered ball, and level shoulders. Those three together force a descending strike on the one club in the bag that wants to be hit on the upswing. Berger's fix is three specific adjustments — tee so that two-thirds of the ball sits above the crown of the driver, position the ball forward to line up with the big toe of the lead foot, and drop the trail shoulder slightly so the spine tilts away from the target. This pre-loads everything an upward strike needs. Berger's swing thought is to "put the clubface on the back of the ball" so the body doesn't lunge ahead. **What it trains.** Address discipline for the driver. **When to use.** Before any range session with driver, or pre-round; especially when the player tees too low, plays the ball center, or stands tall with shoulders level. **Who it helps most.** Beginners through developing players whose address is fighting the driver before the swing starts. **Setup.** Driver, tees, range. **How.** Tee the ball with about two-thirds of the ball above the crown of the driver at address. Move the ball position forward to align with the lead-foot big toe. Take address, then drop the trail shoulder slightly so the spine tilts away from the target. Rehearse the setup 5–10 times without swinging, feeling each piece. Then hit shots from this exact address. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes; the opening block of every driver session or pre-round. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: setup looks correct from the side (high tee, ball forward, trail shoulder slightly lower) before every drive. • Developing: setup is automatic; ball flight pattern shifts higher and longer. **Common mistakes.** Teeing too low (the most common error); playing the ball center (kills the upward strike); standing level-shouldered (no secondary tilt); rehearsing the setup without ever transferring it to a swing.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Daniel Berger (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/simplify-your-tee-shots', 'oga', true),

($drill$Hogan Towel Connection$drill$,
 $drill$Tuck a small towel high in the lead armpit and hit waist-high iron swings keeping the towel pinned — drops the moment your arms disconnect from your chest, forcing a body-led rotational motion.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Josh Zander refines the classic Hogan/Ballard towel drill: you tuck a small towel high in the lead armpit (or trail armpit for strong-grip players, per Zander's nuance) and make 9-to-3 / waist-high swings with a short iron, keeping the towel pinned the whole time. The towel falls the instant your arms come disconnected from your chest — the moment you start hitting "at" the ball with the hands rather than rotating through it with the body. The constraint forces a body-led, synced motion and a connected takeaway, which is exactly what's missing when iron strikes start drifting toey/heely or feel "armsy" under pressure. Zander and Golf Smart Academy both warn the drill is for short, controlled reps — taking it past waist-height with a full swing breaks the connection by design, so don't try to groove that. **What it trains.** Arm-to-torso connection; body-driven rotation through impact. **When to use.** When iron contact gets toey/heely; when the swing feels armsy; as a pre-round reset for connection. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players whose connection breaks under pressure. **Setup.** A small hand towel, a short iron (8 or 9), range. **How.** Place the towel high under your lead armpit (or trail armpit if you have a strong grip). Take address with the short iron. Make 9-to-3 / waist-high swings — keep the towel pinned through the whole motion. If it drops, you disconnected. Sets of 10 half-swings, then a set of normal-length swings carrying the connection feel without the towel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week or as a pre-round reset. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete 10 swings with the towel staying pinned. • Developing/competitive: connection feel carries into full swings (no towel) with better contact. **Common mistakes.** Taking the swing past waist-height with the towel (kills the drill); gripping tight to "hold" the arms together; doing it only with the towel and never transferring; choosing a long iron (a short iron is the right club).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,tempo}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Josh Zander, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com); Hogan/Ballard lineage$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/right-wrong-way-popular-golf-swing-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Como Chair Drill (Anti-Early-Extension)$drill$,
 $drill$Stand to a chair with the seat facing you and knees about an inch from the seat — swing without your knees bumping the chair to feel posture retention through impact, and kill the hip thrust that causes blocks and shanks.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Chris Como uses a chair as the cleanest physical reference for early extension during iron swings — the hip-thrust toward the ball in the downswing that produces thin shots, blocked rights, and shanks because the body has bailed out of posture. With the chair's seat facing you and knees about an inch from the seat edge, you make backswing-and-through rotations with arms crossed; your knees can't contact the chair if your posture stays. If your knees collapse forward and bump the seat, you've early-extended. An advanced variant moves the knees 4–5 inches back, then teaches a more nuanced sequence: in the downswing the knees move *toward* the chair, then both legs straighten while the upper body stays down, until the knees nearly touch at the finish. **What it trains.** Posture retention through impact; correct lower-body action without hip thrust. **When to use.** When ball flight is thin, blocked-right, or shanky and video shows the hips thrusting toward the ball. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with the diagnosis. Casual players often need a simpler weight-shift fix first. **Setup.** A chair with the seat facing you. Stand to a 7-iron with knees about 1 inch from the seat edge, arms crossed for the basic version. **How.** Make backswing-and-through rotations without your knees touching the chair. Knees collapsing in = early extension. For the advanced version, move knees 4–5 inches back and feel the down-swing sequence: knees move toward chair, then both legs straighten while the upper body stays down, knees nearly touching at finish. Rehearse 10–15 times, then hit shots without the chair carrying the feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1×/week on posture work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: knees stay off the chair on most rehearsals; ball flight tightens. • Competitive: advanced version's sequence becomes automatic. **Common mistakes.** Confusing this with a weight-shift drill (it's a posture check); doing it only as rehearsals (need transfer to balls); knees bumping the chair and ignoring it; using it without the diagnosis (other faults need other fixes).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Chris Como, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/chair-drill-cure-early-extension-chris-como/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Stinger / Low Window$drill$,
 $drill$Play the ball a ball-width back, set pressure on the lead leg, make a full wide-arc backswing — then de-loft through impact and slow the arms immediately after to keep the flight low.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Sean Foley (Tiger Woods's former coach) breaks down the stinger as a specific technique, not a guess. The ball moves about one ball-width back of normal, pressure sets on the lead leg, hands are ahead at address. Foley's key correction is to make a wide-arc backswing and *rotate fully* — most amateurs try to feel "punchy" by narrowing the arc, which kills control and contact. Through impact, let the trail wrist extend / lead wrist bow (the DJ-style condition) to de-loft the club, then *slow the arms* immediately after impact. The finish stays low because the hands aren't releasing aggressively — the abbreviated, low finish is the result of the slow-arm move, not a manufactured chop. **What it trains.** Low-trajectory iron technique on demand. **When to use.** Into the wind, under tree branches, on links or firm-fairway approaches, or any time you need to take spin off. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players past basic strike. Beginners should learn the standard iron flight first. **Setup.** Range, any mid-to-long iron (5- through 8-iron is the canonical band). **How.** Address with the ball about one ball-width back of normal, pressure on the lead leg, hands ahead. Make a normal wide-arc backswing and rotate fully — don't shorten it. Through impact, feel the trail wrist extend / lead wrist bow to de-loft. Slow the arms right after impact so the finish stays low and abbreviated. Reps with a 6- or 7-iron; 8–10 balls at a time. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week when working trajectory control. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: produce a clearly lower flight than your stock iron with reasonable contact. • Competitive: stinger on demand, controlled curve, useful into-wind option. **Common mistakes.** Narrowing the backswing (kills contact); not de-lofting through impact (the ball just flies normal-high); a normal aggressive release (sends it high again); ball position too far back (causes a chunk or hot-low-screamer).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Sean Foley, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/tiger-woods-stinger-royal-liverpool/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Knockdown 2-2-2$drill$,
 $drill$Grip down two inches, play the ball two inches back, drop the lead foot back two inches — three simple changes that turn any iron into a controlled three-quarter knockdown for wind or trouble.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Tina Tombs's "2-2-2 method" is a knockdown shot you can dial in without rebuilding your swing. Three explicit setup adjustments — grip down 2 inches, play the ball 2 inches back of normal, drop the lead foot back 2 inches into a slightly open stance — combine with a three-quarter back-and-through swing to produce a delofted, lower-flighted, lower-spin shot that finishes in a controlled position. The grip-down and back-of-center ball position deloft the club at impact; the open stance gives the body room to rotate through; the three-quarter swing controls speed and finish length. Reps grooves a reliable wind/trouble shot you can pull off the rack whenever the situation calls. **What it trains.** Reliable three-quarter knockdown — wind, between-club gaps, low-ceiling trouble. **When to use.** Into wind, low-hanging branches, tough lies, or any time you want a controlled "70%" iron flight. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Casuals often struggle to feel a true three-quarter swing — they hit full and decelerate, which is the opposite of the drill. **Setup.** Range, 7-iron through PW. **How.** For each club: grip down 2 inches on the handle, position the ball 2 inches back from normal, drop the lead foot back 2 inches into a slightly open stance. Make a three-quarter back-and-through swing — finish lower than a full swing. Reps in sets of 8–10 per club, focusing on consistent trajectory and a finish length that matches the abbreviated backswing. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly on trajectory and trouble-shot work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clearly lower flight than your stock iron with consistent contact. • Competitive: knockdown distance and trajectory dialed in — known yardage for the controlled flight. **Common mistakes.** Making a full swing (defeats the three-quarter point); not gripping down (no deloft); decelerating into impact (a half-swing back, full-effort through, ends in mishits); finishing high (the low finish matches the low flight).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{trajectory,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Tina Tombs, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/master-knockdown-shot-2-2-2-method/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tee-Gate Center-Face$drill$,
 $drill$Plant two tees just wider than your clubhead — toe and heel — and swing a mid-iron cleanly through the gate; clipping either side proves you're missing the center of the face, and you can narrow the gate as strike improves.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kellie Stenzel's tee-gate drill gives instant heel/toe feedback in a way that face-tape and impact spray can't beat for simplicity. Two tees planted in the ground flanking the ball, with the gate just slightly wider than your clubhead, force the strike toward the center of the face — if you can't pass cleanly through without knocking a tee, you're missing the center. The audible click of a tee being clipped is unmistakable feedback, and you can tell from which side it came whether you're catching the heel or the toe. Stenzel: "If you're unable to do this without hitting either tee, you will likely not hit the center of the face." Tightening the gate as strike improves makes the drill scale with the player. **What it trains.** Center-face strike location (heel-to-toe). **When to use.** When face-tape or impact spray shows scattered strike location; when distance and dispersion are inconsistent despite otherwise-decent contact. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal; gate width scales with skill. **Setup.** Range, a mid-iron, two tees, and the discipline to listen for the click. **How.** Plant the two tees flanking the ball with the gate slightly wider than your clubhead. Hit shots — clean pass through = center contact. A toe click = swing was too far from the ball or face was open; a heel click = swing too close or face shut. Sets of 8–10. Narrow the gate slightly as you improve. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on contact work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: pass through a generous gate on 6 of 10. • Developing: 8 of 10 at a tight gate; flight dispersion tightens. • Competitive: 9 of 10 at a gate barely wider than the clubhead. **Common mistakes.** Setting the gate too wide so you never get feedback; ignoring which side you clip (the pattern is the diagnosis); swinging tentatively to avoid clicks (kills the swing); not tightening the gate as you improve.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{contact,dispersion}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Kellie Stenzel, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/how-to-hit-center-clubface-consistently-kellie-stenzel/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Feet-Together Iron$drill$,
 $drill$With feet touching and a 7-iron, make full swings — the narrow base falls you over the moment arms and body disconnect, and Pete Cowen says nine of ten amateurs benefit at the cost of about ten percent distance.$drill$,
 15, 'approach', '{range}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Pete Cowen — long-time coach to Henrik Stenson, Brooks Koepka, and others — uses the feet-together iron drill as a universal balance and sync corrective. Feet touching (or within an inch) gives you a deliberately tiny base. Now make full swings with a 7-iron. The narrow base punishes any disconnect between arm-swing and body rotation — if you swing out of sync, you literally fall over. The drill grooves balance, swing-center stability, and arm-body sync without restricting club speed; you're still making a full swing, you just can't afford to be sloppy. Cowen's framing: roughly 90% of amateurs benefit from this drill, and the trade-off is about 10% distance loss in exchange for major consistency gains. Tee the ball initially for confidence, then hit from the turf. **What it trains.** Balance; swing-center stability; arm-body sync under full swing speed. **When to use.** When ball-flight pattern is two-way (off-balance finishes, varying low-points, "armsy" lunges); a great session-opener. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal. **Setup.** 7-iron, range, balls (tee initially, then turf). **How.** Stand with feet touching or within an inch. Tee the ball at first for confidence; switch to off the turf when balanced. Make full swings — accept the distance loss. Cowen prescribes sets of 10; finish by returning to your normal stance and feeling the difference. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1×/week or as a session-opener. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: complete sets of 10 without falling; flight tightens. • Developing/competitive: full-speed swings with no balance loss; sync carries into the normal-stance swings that follow. **Common mistakes.** Keeping the swing tentative to stay balanced (defeats the point — go full); spreading the feet slightly to "cheat" the balance; skipping the transfer back to normal stance; treating the distance loss as a problem (it's part of the trade).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Pete Cowen (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/approach-shots/pete-cowen-golf-coach-swing-feet-together-drill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Toe-Down Chip$drill$,
 $drill$Stand close enough that the heel of your wedge rides off the ground and only the toe touches — the diagonal leading edge resists digging on tight lies and into-grain, and the stroke is pure putter-rocking.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Phil Mickelson's toe-down chip (Golf.com) is the cleanest answer to the tight-lie problem that wrecks amateurs: a normal chip from a hard, short lie wants to dig, and a dig is a chunk or a skull. Standing closer to the ball so the heel of the wedge rises off the ground and only the toe touches creates a slight diagonal leading edge that resists digging — the club rocks across the surface instead of catching turf. The motion is pure putting: grip and posture mirror your putting stroke, shoulder-rocking, no wrist hinge. Ball pops over the fringe and rolls out like a putt. It's a single self-contained technique with a clear "when" — tight lies, hard ground, into-grain — and it doesn't depend on any larger wedge framework. **What it trains.** Clean contact on tight or into-grain lies by changing the club's effective lie angle. **When to use.** Ball just off the green on a tight lie, or anywhere the grain grows toward you — anywhere a normal chip would dig. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who have a steady putting stroke and can read the popped-out roll. **Setup.** Wedge (any loft works), tight lie just off the green. **How.** Stand closer to the ball than a normal chip — close enough that the heel of the wedge sits off the ground and only the toe makes contact at address. Grip and posture mirror your putting stroke. Make a shoulder-rocking, no-wrist stroke — like a long putt. The ball pops over the fringe and rolls out. Reps: 10–15 chips from a tight-lie spot. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, weekly when working tight-lie short game. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean contact on most reps; ball rolls predictably like a putt. • Competitive: dial in finish distance from tight lies with no chunks or skulls. **Common mistakes.** Adding wrist hinge (kills the putting-stroke simplicity); using it from a cushy lie (the technique solves a tight-lie problem); standing normal distance from the ball (the toe-down setup IS the drill); expecting flight (it's a low roller, not a high stop).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Phil Mickelson (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/phil-mickelson-chip-video-toe-down/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Open-Face Flop (Fowler)$drill$,
 $drill$Open the face before you grip it, set weight forward and ball forward, hold the face open at the top, then commit fully — the high-soft flop for short-sided pins where the ball has to stop on a dime.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Rickie Fowler's flop technique (Golf.com) makes the high-soft stop a repeatable shot rather than a desperate stab. Two non-negotiables: open the clubface *first* and then take your grip — this keeps the face open through the swing, exposing maximum loft and bounce. Set up with weight forward (lead side) and ball forward, and hold the face open at the top of the backswing so bounce stays exposed. Then swing through with weight staying forward and *commit fully* — hesitation kills the drill, producing chunks (you bail at impact) or skulls (you flip to "help" the ball up). Fowler's coaching point is that "trust" is the variable; the technique is sound and the bounce will save you if you commit. **What it trains.** High, soft-landing flop with maximum stopping power. **When to use.** Short-sided pins with no green to work with, when you must stop the ball quickly with little room to roll. **Who it helps most.** Competitive players. It's a high-risk shot; developing players are usually better served by a putt-from-fringe or bump-and-run. **Setup.** Short-game green with a target you can short-side, a high-loft wedge (58° or 60°), 15–25 balls. **How.** Open the clubface to your desired loft *before* you take your grip; grip with the face already open. Address with weight forward, ball forward (off the lead foot), and a slightly open stance. Make a full back-and-through swing — hold the face open at the top, weight staying forward, *commit through impact*. Reps: 10–15 from a good lie focusing on commitment, then 10 from a tight lie. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes, 1×/week when working short-game peak distance. **Success metric.** Competitive: ball lands soft, stops quickly, no chunks or skulls when you commit. **Common mistakes.** Gripping first and then opening the face (the face closes back through impact); decelerating at impact (chunk or skull); shifting weight back to "lift" the ball (the loft does the lifting); trying it for the first time on a critical course shot.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Rickie Fowler (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/short-game/rickie-fowler-tips-hitting-flop-shot/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Bump-and-Run$drill$,
 $drill$Pick a lower-loft iron, narrow stance, ball back to the trail toe, hands ahead — the lowest-risk shot from just off the green, hops on the green early and rolls like a putt.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Brendon Elliott (PGA.com) lays out the bump-and-run as the safety-first short-game shot — a low chip that gets the ball on the ground early and lets it roll out like a putt. Five elements: a lower-loft club (typically a 9-iron or PW), a narrow stance, ball position back toward the trail toe, hands ahead at address to promote a downward strike, and a short, smooth backswing with a matching follow-through. The setup pre-loads the downward strike that catches the ball cleanly and produces low trajectory; once on the ground, the lower loft gives you maximum roll. Compared to a flop or even a higher chip, the bump-and-run has fewer ways to go wrong — fewer chunks, fewer skulls — which is exactly why it's the bread-and-butter shot when conditions allow. **What it trains.** Low-trajectory ball-back chip that hops on the green early and rolls. **When to use.** Tight to the green with green to work with, no hazard to carry, firm conditions — the standard low-risk play. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Strongest fit for beginners and casual players who struggle with higher-loft chips. **Setup.** Short-game green with green to work with, a 9-iron or PW. **How.** Narrow stance. Ball back toward the trail toe. Hands ahead of the ball at address. Short backswing, short matched follow-through, smooth tempo. Don't try to "lift" — the loft on the iron does the lifting; you swing down through the ball. Land the ball a yard or two onto the green and let it roll. Sets of 10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on short-game. **Success metric, by level.** • Beginner/casual: clean contact on most; ball reaches the hole's general area. • Developing: ball lands consistently and rolls to within a few feet. • Competitive: precise distance control. **Common mistakes.** Using too much loft (defeats the purpose); ball position too forward (chunks); hands not ahead (scoops); long, loose backswing (decel comes next); trying to fly it all the way to the hole.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Brendon Elliott, PGA (PGA.com)$drill$, 'https://www.pga.com/story/a-six-step-guide-to-master-the-bump-and-run-chip', 'oga', true),

($drill$Dan Grieve Tee-Peg Pitch$drill$,
 $drill$Tee a ball at driver height with a 54° or 56° wedge — try to clip the ball cleanly without disturbing the tee, contacting the third or fourth groove for a shallow high-spin strike instead of a dig.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Master Professional Dan Grieve (Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach) built a constraint drill that forces a shallow, "clipping" pitch strike instead of the digging chunk that ruins most amateur pitches. You tee a ball at driver height, rest it on the tee, and hit it with a 54° or 56° wedge. The instruction is to hover the club at the ball's base and try to clip the ball off the tee *without disturbing the tee*. The contact should be on the third or fourth groove of the face — meaning you've brushed the ball cleanly rather than driven down into it. The drill is brutal-but-clean feedback: knock the tee and you dug; clip cleanly and you'll see a higher-spin pitch leave the face. Repetition grooves the shallow strike for 20–60 yard pitches from a good lie. **What it trains.** Clipping the ball (vs digging) on pitch shots — high-spin contact via the third/fourth groove. **When to use.** 20–60 yard pitch shots from a good lie where you want height and spin rather than a divot-driven flighted shot. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who chunk pitches or struggle to put spin on the ball. **Setup.** A short-game green or range, a 54° or 56° wedge, tees, balls. Tee balls at driver height with the ball resting on the tee. **How.** Hover the wedge at the ball's base (level with where you'd normally address). Make a pitch-length swing trying to clip the ball off the tee without knocking the tee over. Contact target: the third or fourth groove of the face. Sets of 10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, weekly when working pitching. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clip without disturbing the tee on most balls. • Competitive: every shot leaves the tee standing, ball flies high and spins. **Common mistakes.** Teeing too low (no constraint); hitting down on the tee (defeats the point); rushing without checking the tee after each shot; using a 60° (the loft makes it easier — Grieve specifies 54° or 56° because they're the more common pitch clubs).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact,trajectory}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Dan Grieve, PGA Master Pro (Golf Monthly)$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/this-is-my-favourite-drill-dan-grieve-shares-the-secret-to-hitting-better-pitch-shots', 'oga', true),

($drill$Uphill Bunker Slope-Match$drill$,
 $drill$Align your shoulders to the upslope so the club chases the slope instead of digging, play the ball forward, dig your feet in — expect a high short flight, and step down a club for longer carries instead of swinging harder.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA Master Professional Kellie Stenzel (Women's Golf) teaches uphill bunker shots as a matter of matching the body to the slope rather than fighting it. Align your shoulders to the slope — this effectively adds loft and allows the club to chase up the slope instead of digging into it. Play the ball forward in the stance, dig your feet in for footing, make a regular swing, and keep the legs quiet so the club can follow the slope. Trying to lift the ball or muscle it up actively makes it worse because it forces the club down into the sand. The result is a high, short flight that stops quickly — and for longer carries, the answer is to drop down a club rather than swinging harder. **What it trains.** Bunker technique on upslope lies. **When to use.** Greenside bunker with an upslope between you and the green / pin. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Beginners need flat-lie bunker mechanics first (Bunker Sand-Entry, Bunker No-Ball Splash). **Setup.** A greenside bunker with an upslope, a sand/lob wedge. **How.** Address. Align shoulders to the slope (left shoulder higher than right for a right-hander). Play the ball forward in the stance. Dig your feet in for solid footing. Make a regular swing — keep the legs quiet and let the club chase up the slope. The ball comes out high and short. For longer carries, take a less-lofted club; do not swing harder. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, monthly bunker work or whenever an upslope is on the practice agenda. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean splash from upslope lies, ball reaches the green. • Competitive: distance control by club selection. **Common mistakes.** Staying level-shouldered (kills the splash, leaves you in the bunker); trying to lift the ball; swinging harder for longer carries (use a less-lofted club instead); ball position too far back.$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Kellie Stenzel, PGA Master Pro (Women's Golf)$drill$, 'https://womensgolf.com/uphill-lie-bunker/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Downhill Bunker Slope-Match$drill$,
 $drill$Match knees, hips, and shoulders to the downslope, hinge steeply for a steep attack (downhill kills loft), keep the loft on low through impact — accept a lower flight with more roll and play for the up-and-down, not the pin.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Craig Fricker (National Club Golfer) teaches the downhill bunker shot as the mirror of the uphill — match the body to the slope rather than fighting it. Knees, hips, and shoulders all tilt to follow the downslope, which naturally moves more weight to the lead side. Because a downhill lie kills loft, the answer isn't to add it through wrist action (which produces chunks); the answer is a steep wrist hinge on the backswing for a steep, abbreviated attack that delivers the club at the right angle on the slope. Through impact, "keep the loft on low" — don't let the club release past the hands the way you would on a flat-lie splash. The realistic target is an up-and-down, not a tight pin; you'll get a lower flight with more roll, and that's correct. **What it trains.** Bunker technique on downslope lies. **When to use.** Greenside bunker with the ball on a downslope. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. **Setup.** A greenside bunker with a downslope, a sand/lob wedge. **How.** Address. Match knees, hips, and shoulders to the slope angle (weight ends up on the lead side as a result). Hinge the wrists steeply on the backswing to create a steep attack angle. Through impact, keep the hands ahead and "the loft on low" — do not flip or release past the hands. Expect a lower flight with more roll. Pick a landing spot well short of the pin. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes when downhill bunker is on the practice agenda. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: clean splash, ball gets out of the bunker. • Competitive: ball-out percentage near 100%; distance control by landing spot. **Common mistakes.** Trying to add loft by flipping (chunks); standing level (kills the splash); not hinging steeply enough (shallow attack on a downhill = thinning); aiming for the pin (the geometry usually won't allow it — aim for the up-and-down).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Craig Fricker, PGA (National Club Golfer)$drill$, 'https://www.nationalclubgolfer.com/golf-tips/short-game/golf-tips-downhill-bunker-shot/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Belly-Wedge from the Collar$drill$,
 $drill$Grip a wedge like your putter, hover the leading edge at the ball's equator, and use a putting stroke to deliberately thin it — the leading-edge contact rolls the ball forward when a putter would snag and a chip would chunk.$drill$,
 10, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Stan Utley (SwingU Master Faculty, Golf Digest Top 50 in America) teaches the belly wedge as the answer to the cut-line problem: the ball sitting on the boundary between fringe and rough, where a putter snags on the longer grass and a normal chip risks a chunk or skull off the awkward lie. The trick is to use the wedge's leading edge with a putting stroke — grip the wedge the way you grip your putter, hover the leading edge level with the equator (middle) of the ball, and use your normal putting stroke. The contact deliberately "thins" the ball with the leading edge, which creates top-spin and a controlled, putting-style roll. Because you're hovering, the bounce slides over the grass behind the ball rather than catching it. It's a single self-contained technique with a clear "when" — collar lies. **What it trains.** Putt-with-a-wedge escape technique for fringe/collar lies. **When to use.** Ball sits on the cut line between fringe and rough, or against the collar — putter won't slide through cleanly but the lie isn't bad enough for a true chip. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who get caught in awkward lies. **Setup.** A spot on the practice green with fringe and rough adjacent (or any spot where the ball can rest against longer grass). Wedge (sand or pitching). **How.** Grip the wedge with your putting grip. Take your normal putting posture. Hover the leading edge of the wedge level with the equator of the ball (NOT behind the ball on the ground). Make your normal putting stroke and strike the ball at its middle — deliberately thin it with the leading edge. The ball rolls forward like a putt. Reps: 10–15 from a collar lie. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes when working short-game escapes. **Success metric.** Ball rolls cleanly off the leading edge, like a putt. **Common mistakes.** Trying to make a chipping motion (you'll catch the grass); not hovering the club (defeats the slide-over-grass benefit); aiming below the ball's equator (chunk it); using too much swing length (it's a putt-stroke).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{chip_contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Stan Utley (SwingU)$drill$, 'https://clubhouse.swingu.com/instruction/belly-your-wedge-to-escape-this-tricky-spot-2/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Putt-from-Fringe$drill$,
 $drill$Putt across the green to the far fringe and back as a pace primer — Shot Scope data says scratch golfers go 81% up-and-down putting from fringe vs 8–22% wedging, and the under-used putter is the highest-percentage play whenever the lie's clean.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game,putting}', '{beginner,casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** PGA professional Neil Marr (Golf Monthly Top 50 Coach), drawing on Shot Scope's tour-of-millions-of-shots dataset, makes the data-backed case that amateurs dramatically under-use the putter from off the green. The numbers: scratch golfers get up-and-down 81% of the time putting from the fringe vs 8–22% with any wedge; a 25-handicap goes 62% up-and-down putting vs 21% / 22% / 9% / 8% with PW/GW/SW/LW. The technique itself is simple — three questions decide whether to putt: can the putter strike the ball cleanly through any grass behind it, how much fringe (5+ ft is hard to judge), and what's the fringe condition? If you green-light it, take a normal putt from the fringe distance and add a touch more pace to account for deceleration crossing the fringe. The drill builds the speed feel: putt balls from one side of the green across to the opposite fringe (no cup) and back, building fringe-pace feel without scoring noise. **What it trains.** The default fringe-putt mechanic and fringe-distance pace feel. **When to use.** Ball on or just off the fringe with a clean lie, no significant fringe hump, less than 5 ft of fringe to cross. Don't use when there's long grass behind the ball or undulating fringe. **Who it helps most.** Every level — the Shot Scope data argues all skill levels under-use the putter from the fringe. **Setup.** A practice green with accessible fringe, putter, balls. **How.** Place several balls along one side of the green. Putt each to the opposite fringe (no cup), then putt them back. Add a touch more pace than you'd use on the green to account for fringe deceleration. Rotate distance, repeat. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: balls reach the far fringe consistently. • Developing: balls finish within a few feet of the far fringe. • Competitive: tight pace control across varying distances. **Common mistakes.** Putting through long grass that snags (use a wedge instead); ignoring fringe condition; not adjusting pace for the fringe crossing; defaulting to a wedge from a clean lie because "that's what you do."$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,lag}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Neil Marr, PGA (Golf Monthly); Shot Scope dataset$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/when-should-i-putt-from-the-fringe-this-data-will-challenge-your-short-game-mindset', 'oga', true),

($drill$Chalk-Line Start Line$drill$,
 $drill$Snap a chalk line along a straight 20-foot putt and roll balls with their painted line aligned to the chalk — the ball's stripe should roll end-over-end along the chalk, and any drift tells you exactly how the face missed.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Bryson DeChambeau's chalk-line start-line drill (Golf.com) is the most honest feedback available for putter-face control. Snap a chalk line on a relatively straight ~20-foot putt and align each ball's painted line to the chalk. As you roll the ball, the stripe traces a continuous record of its path — if the stripe stays on the chalk, the face was square at impact; any drift left or right shows exactly how much the face missed and in which direction. DeChambeau: it lets him "calibrate my start line, so I know the ball is rolling off the face the way I want it to." Unlike a gate drill (pass/fail at one point) or a tee track (constrains start direction), the chalk line shows the entire roll — the diagnosis is continuous and unambiguous. **What it trains.** Face-square contact + start line — proof that the ball actually starts where you aimed. **When to use.** When putts that look pure miss right or left of the intended line — diagnostic for face-control errors invisible at address. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Beginners get little signal — they need solid contact first. **Setup.** A practice green with a relatively straight ~20-foot section, chalk, line snapper (or a string and chalk), a putter, balls with painted alignment lines. **How.** Snap a chalk line along the straight putt line. Set each ball with its painted line aligned to the chalk. Roll putts — watch whether the stripe rolls end-over-end ALONG the chalk. Drift left = pull/closed face; drift right = push/open face. Reps: sets of 5–10. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on stroke work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: most balls track the chalk for the first several feet. • Competitive: balls track the chalk for the entire roll. **Common mistakes.** Doing it on a breaking putt (the chalk only works on a straight line); not aligning the painted ball line to the chalk; ignoring the drift pattern (the drift is the diagnosis); rolling too fast (the chalk smudges quickly — use one or two rolls per chalk pass).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Bryson DeChambeau (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/pre-round-drill-us-open-champ-sync-up-putting/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Eye-Drop Setup Check$drill$,
 $drill$Take your putting setup, hold a second ball at the bridge of your nose, and drop it — if it lands on the address ball your eyes are over the ball, outside means too far away, inside means too close.$drill$,
 5, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Tiger Woods's eye-drop check (documented by Golf Digest at the 2023 Masters) tests the single setup precondition for being able to see the line accurately: are your eyes directly over the ball? With eyes off the ball, you're looking at the target line from an angle that distorts both direction and distance — you can have a great stroke and still putt to the wrong spot. The test is dead simple: take your normal putting address over a ball, hold a *second* ball up to the bridge of your nose, and drop it. If your eyes are correctly positioned, the dropped ball lands on top of (or just inside) the address ball. Lands outside = you're standing too far from the ball; lands inside = too close. Adjust posture/distance, repeat. Tiger drops 5 times before he hits a putt — about two minutes, no strokes. **What it trains.** Eye position at address. **When to use.** Before any putting session or pre-round when alignment feels off; when you keep starting putts on lines you didn't intend; periodic setup recalibration. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players — universal. Beginners can use it; they just won't always know which adjustment to make. **Setup.** A practice green, a putter, two balls. **How.** Address a ball on the green as you would for a real putt. Hold the second ball at the bridge of your nose, just under your eyes. Drop it. Note where it lands relative to the address ball. Adjust posture / distance from the ball if the dropped ball lands outside or inside, and repeat. Tiger's prescription: 5 drops before each putt during a setup-check session. **Time & frequency.** 5 minutes; before any putting practice or as a pre-round check. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: dropped ball lands on or near the address ball most reps. • Developing/competitive: dropped ball lands precisely on the address ball every time. **Common mistakes.** Holding the second ball too far from your nose (artificial position); skipping the adjustment after a miss; doing it without an actual putt afterward (no transfer); thinking eye position is "set forever" (it drifts).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{start_line}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Tiger Woods (Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.golfdigest.com/story/tiger-woods-masters-2023-putting-drill-eyes', 'oga', true),

($drill$Chopsticks Connected-Stroke$drill$,
 $drill$Place an alignment rod under each forearm with the rods crossing at your sternum — the rods press on top of both arms and physically constrain the wrists, so the stroke becomes body-driven and connected.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** GOLF Top 100 Teacher Kevin Sprecher's "chopsticks" drill, which he credits to Dr. Craig Farnsworth, is the cleanest physical reference for a connected, body-driven putting stroke. You place one alignment rod under each forearm so each rod runs across the forearm and extends out behind you. The lead-arm rod crosses *over* the trail-arm rod at your sternum, forming an X. Your hands grip the putter normally, but the rods press lightly on the top of both forearms at the same place. Now you can't independently flick the wrists or break the arm triangle without dislodging the rods — the constraint forces the arms to move *together* with the torso, killing wrist independence and shoulder/arm disconnection. Sprecher: it "gives you the sensation of being more 'connected' in the stroke and reduces excessive wrist motion … feel you are using your body more and hands less." **What it trains.** A connected, body-driven stroke; eliminates wrist independence. **When to use.** When putts feel handsy or jerky; when stroke quality breaks down under pressure; when distance control is inconsistent across short sessions (a sign of wrist independence). **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players. Beginners need a baseline stroke first; this drill diagnoses wrist independence in an existing stroke. **Setup.** A practice green, putter, two alignment rods. **How.** Hold both rods. Slide the trail-arm rod under your trail forearm. Slide the lead-arm rod under your lead forearm so it crosses *over* the trail rod at your sternum (forming an X). Grip the putter normally — hands under the rods, rods pressing lightly on both forearms at similar points. Make putting strokes. The rods constrain the arms to move together with the torso. Sets of 10–15 strokes; then remove the rods and putt 5 balls trying to preserve the connected feel. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week on stroke work. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: rods stay in place across full strokes; feel of "body, not hands." • Developing/competitive: connected feel transfers to rod-free strokes; distance control improves. **Common mistakes.** Gripping too tight to hold the rods (kills feel); not crossing the rods (loses the constraint); skipping the rod-free transfer putts; expecting it to fix line/aim (it fixes connection — pair with start-line work).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Dr. Craig Farnsworth / Kevin Sprecher, GOLF Top 100 (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/simple-putting-drill-reduce-wrist-motion/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tee-in-the-Cup Speed Pair$drill$,
 $drill$Push a tee into the back lip of the cup and try to hit it on uphill 5-to-10-footers — then move the tee to the front lip and try to drop downhillers in without touching it. Uphill firmness, downhill touch, two paces, one drill.$drill$,
 15, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Brad Faxon (8-time PGA Tour winner, considered one of the best putters ever and Rory McIlroy's putting coach) uses a single tee in the cup to train two opposite paces in one short drill. Uphill set: push a tee into the back lip of the cup so it stands about an inch above the surface. From ~5–10 feet straight uphill, roll putts trying to *hit* the tee on the way in — forces enough firmness that the ball won't die on the lip. Downhill set: move the tee to the *front* lip; from straight downhill the same distance, roll putts trying to drop them in *without* touching the tee — forces a soft, dying-speed roll. Faxon's exact direction: "On uphill putts, put a tee in the back of the hole … hit the ball hard enough so that your ball will hit that tee on the uphill putt. Now on the downhill putt, it's the opposite … I want the ball to roll in and not touch the tee." The pair builds firmness-vs-touch as opposite skills, each with a binary make-or-fail signal. **What it trains.** Speed control on slope — uphill firmness vs downhill touch. **When to use.** When uphill putts come up short or downhill putts run by; when speed control on sloped greens is the persistent leak. **Who it helps most.** Casual through competitive players who have access to a sloped practice green. Beginners can do it but speed-on-slope is a higher-level diagnostic. **Setup.** A practice green with a clear slope and at least one hole on it, a tee, putter, balls. **How.** Uphill: push tee into the back lip of the cup so it stands ~1 inch above the surface. From 5–10 feet straight uphill, roll putts trying to *hit* the tee on the way in. 5–10 reps. Downhill: move tee to the front lip. From straight downhill 5–10 feet, roll putts trying to drop them in *without* touching the tee. 5–10 reps. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes, 1–2×/week. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: hit the back tee on most uphill makes; don't touch the front tee on most downhill makes. • Developing: separation between paces is clean. • Competitive: both paces dialed and repeatable. **Common mistakes.** Setting the tee on flat ground (the slope is half the drill); choosing too long a distance (10 ft max); chasing one pace while neglecting the other; not pairing them in the same session.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,distance_control}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Brad Faxon (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/one-best-ever-putters-placing-tee-cup-why/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Acceleration Coin$drill$,
 $drill$Balance a coin on the putter's back cavity and stroke — the coin should fly off backward at the transition from backstroke to forward stroke, proving peak speed at the START of the forward stroke (the pro pattern), not at impact (the jab).$drill$,
 10, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Phil Kenyon (Rory McIlroy's putting coach, GOLF Top 100 Teacher) uses a coin on the putter's back cavity to give immediate visible feedback about *when* peak acceleration happens in the stroke. The good-putter pattern is peak putter-head speed at the *start* of the forward stroke (right at the transition from backstroke to forward stroke), with the head decelerating smoothly into impact. The amateur fault is the opposite — slow back, slow forward, then jab at the ball — which produces hits, mishits, and short-putt yips. Balance a coin on the back cavity of the putter. Take your normal stroke. The coin should fly off the putter *backward* (away from the target) right at the transition — that confirms peak acceleration happens at the start of the forward stroke. If the coin only falls near impact, you're accelerating too late (jabbing). If it falls during the backstroke, your takeaway is too fast. *(Despite the drill being from Kenyon, no Kenyon framework or vocabulary is needed — the coin gives all the feedback you need.)* **What it trains.** Tempo and acceleration timing in the putting stroke. **When to use.** When you decelerate into impact (the classic short-putt yip-precursor); when the stroke jabs rather than rolls; when tempo feels rushed. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players with a coachable baseline stroke. **Setup.** A practice green, a putter (mallet works best — flat back cavity holds the coin reliably; many blades work too), balls, a small coin. **How.** Balance the coin on the back cavity of the putter. Take a normal stroke. Watch where the coin flies off — backward at transition (correct), at impact (late = jab), during the backstroke (takeaway too fast). Adjust tempo, repeat until the coin reliably ejects backward at transition. Sets of 10 stroke-only reps; then putt 5 balls trying to reproduce the feel without the coin. **Time & frequency.** 10 minutes, 1×/week on tempo work. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: coin ejects backward at transition on most strokes. • Competitive: every stroke ejects the coin backward; strokes without the coin feel acceleration-at-transition. **Common mistakes.** Using a blade with no back cavity (coin won't balance); coin too small (no signal); skipping the transfer to balls; treating it as a once-and-done (the feel erodes).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{tempo,contact}', 'blocked',
 $drill$Phil Kenyon (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/putting/10-pga-tour-player-approved-putting-drills-you-can-try/', 'oga', true),


($drill$Out of Bounds Driver Game$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 5 straights, 5 draws, 5 fades to a 20-yard fairway with +5 / 0 / −5 scoring for fairway / safe miss / OB — your total tells you which shape to play on the course.$drill$,
 25, 'off_tee', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Alistair Davies (Golf Monthly Top 25 Coach) reframes range driver practice from "hit balls and watch shape" to "score each shape against a real consequence." Imagine a 20-yard-wide fairway, OB on either side. Hit five straights, five draws, five fades. Each ball that finds the fairway earns +5; a ball on the "safe" side (off the fairway but not OB) is 0; a ball OB is −5. The penalty changes the math you do on the tee: a shape that lands 4 of 5 fairways but 1 OB scores +15 (4×5 − 5), whereas a shape that lands 3 of 5 fairways with 2 safe misses scores +15 too — and one of them is far safer at a tournament. Per-shape totals tell you which shape your swing actually owns under pressure (consistent positive) versus which one is the lottery ticket (high mean, big variance). "The shot shape that gives you the highest score is the one you should then look to play when you face a similar scenario on the course." **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already shape the ball on demand and need to pick the shape they actually play on a real tee. Not for beginners — needs three working shapes and a usable corridor. **Setup.** A driving range with visible distance markers; pick two landmarks (sticks, flags, trees) to define a 20-yard corridor at driver-carry distance. Driver, 15 balls. Scorecard or notes app. **How.** From the same tee, hit 5 straight, 5 draw, 5 fade — full routine on each, no do-overs. Score each ball +5 fairway / 0 safe side / −5 OB. Tally three per-shape totals (each −25 to +25) and an overall (−75 to +75). **Time & frequency.** 20–25 minutes weekly during a tee-shot block; the per-shape pattern is the data, the overall is the score-to-beat. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: positive total; one shape clearly highest. • Competitive: overall ≥ +25; the highest shape repeats session to session. **Common mistakes.** Skipping the routine; "trying harder" on the OB side to recover (drop the shape, don't push it); 30-yard corridor (no useful signal — a real fairway is ~30 m); calling close misses "safe" when they're actually OB by your landmarks (be honest).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,start_line,trajectory}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Alistair Davies, Golf Monthly Top 25 Coach$drill$, 'https://www.golfmonthly.com/tips/driving-range-games-168172', 'oga', true),

($drill$Flagstick Challenge$drill$,
 $drill$Pick one approach distance between 60 and 130 yards, hit 20 balls, and count how many finish within a flagstick of the target — a single repeatable number that's brutal to fake.$drill$,
 20, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw (PhD, MSc, PGA Pro — Golf Insider UK) built the Flagstick Challenge to fix the highest-leverage scoring distance — 60 to 130 yards, where amateurs lose the most strokes per round versus Tour. Pick one target in that band, define the success zone as "within a flagstick length of the target" (roughly 8 feet), and hit 20 balls with a full routine. One point per ball inside the zone, zero outside. The maximum is 20; your number is what you record and try to beat next session. The single-distance focus is deliberate — it's a clean signal on dispersion at that distance, not a smeared average across your whole bag. As you grow, narrow the distance ladder by 10 yards or tighten the zone to a putter length. **Who it helps most.** Casual players who never measure wedge/short-iron accuracy and don't know their dispersion at 80 yards; developing players plateaued on GIR; competitive players auditing scoring-zone consistency. **Setup.** A range with visible distance markers or a launch monitor; wedges/short irons covering 60–130 yards; 20 balls; scorecard. Pick ONE target distance and commit to it for the session. **How.** Imagine an 8-foot circle around the target (a flagstick length). Run your full routine on every ball, then score: 1 if it finishes inside the circle, 0 outside. Note where misses tend to finish (short, long, left, right) — pattern data, not just the count. Tally /20; record the score AND the distance you chose. To progress, narrow the success zone to a putter length, or shorten the target distance band. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes weekly during a scoring-zone block; rotate distances across sessions. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6 of 20 at 90 yds. • Developing: 10 of 20 at 100 yds; consistent miss direction. • Competitive: 14 of 20 at 120 yds; misses tight around the zone. **Common mistakes.** Rotating distances within a single 20-ball set (smears the data); skipping the routine; calling close-but-out misses "in" (be honest); never recording the score (no benchmark to beat).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, MSc, PGA Pro (Golf Insider UK)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-practice-games-long-game/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Iron Scoring Game$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 20 balls each to three self-chosen distances — 3 points inside a flagstick, 1 point inside three flagsticks, 0 outside — for a 180-point dispersion benchmark across your scoring zones.$drill$,
 30, 'approach', '{range,sim}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw's Iron Scoring Game expands the Flagstick Challenge into a three-distance dispersion benchmark. Pick three distances you'll face on the course (e.g., 90, 130, 170 yards); hit 20 balls to each. Inner zone — inside a flagstick length (~8 ft) — scores 3 points. Outer zone — between one and three flagsticks (8 to 21 ft) — scores 1 point. Outside both zones is 0. Maximum score is 180 (60 × 3); a Tour player would push above 120; a developing player getting to 60 is real progress. The tiered scoring is what makes it a scoring game rather than a hit-or-miss test: you can lose ground by drifting fat misses outside both zones AND gain ground by tightening your distribution toward dead-center. Shaw explicitly tells you to note shot finishes and miss directions — the pattern is half the value. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who own iron contact and want a real benchmark across the whole scoring zone, not just one distance. Best with a launch monitor or sim; visible range markers work if calibrated. **Setup.** Pick three target distances spanning your scoring zone (short / mid / long). Mark or visualize an 8-ft inner circle and a 21-ft outer circle at each. Three irons or wedges to match. 60 balls. Scorecard with three columns. **How.** Hit 20 balls per distance in blocks (not rotating ball-to-ball — this drill is a dispersion test, not a randomization test; randomization lives in `random` mode). Score 3 / 1 / 0. After 60 balls total your three columns and combined. Note WHERE misses tend to finish — short, long, left, right — that's the actionable read. **Time & frequency.** 30 minutes weekly to monthly as a calibration benchmark; not a daily drill. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 60+ of 180; pattern identifies one distance to focus on. • Competitive: 100+ of 180; miss direction tight, not scattered. **Common mistakes.** Rotating clubs ball-to-ball (different drill); inventing distances on the fly (commit and write them down); only counting inner-zone hits (the 1-point band is the dispersion data); changing distances between sessions so you can't compare scores.$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{dispersion,distance_control,start_line}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, MSc, PGA Pro (Golf Insider UK)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-insider-practice-system/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Chipping Zone Challenge$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 10 chips from 10, 15, or 20 yards into three concentric rings around the hole — 2 points inside the closest, 1 in the next, 0 outside — and chase the 20-point target.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw (PhD, MSc, PGA Pro — Golf Insider UK) builds his chipping benchmark around three concentric rings the radius of a standard 9-iron. Pick a chip distance (10, 15, or 20 yards); hit 10 balls. The article specifies a 20-point target out of 10 shots — the OGA convention to hit that ceiling is 2 points for balls inside the closest ring, 1 for the next, 0 outside. The ring layout matters more than the exact point math: at 9-iron-length radius (~3 ft inner, ~6 ft middle), the target zones are tight enough to expose dispersion that "near the hole" hides. Shaw's coaching prompt is explicit: track WHERE misses finish — short, long, left, right — because the pattern tells you whether the issue is club selection (distance bias) or strike (lateral bias). The 20-point ceiling makes 14 a strong developing benchmark and 16+ a sign you've earned a step up to 15 or 20 yards. **Who it helps most.** Every level. The 10-yard variant is excellent for casual players grooving basic chip mechanics; 15 and 20 are the developing/competitive proving grounds. **Setup.** Practice short-game green; one chip distance you'll commit to for all 10 balls; a 9-iron laid horizontally to lay out three concentric rings around the hole (inner ~3 ft, middle ~6 ft, outer ~9 ft). 10 balls, your favorite chipping wedge. Scorecard. **How.** Hit 10 chips from your chosen distance. Score each: 2 inside the inner ring, 1 in the middle, 0 outside. Tally /20 and note miss pattern. Beat your score next session; move up to the next distance when you regularly hit 16+. *(Per-zone values inferred from the article's "20-point target from 10 shots" — Shaw doesn't spell point values explicitly.)* **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes weekly. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 8 of 20 at 10 yds. • Developing: 14 of 20 at 15 yds; misses concentrated, not scattered. • Competitive: 16+ at 20 yds. **Common mistakes.** Rotating distances mid-set (10 balls at one distance is the drill); calling the middle ring "close enough" without scoring it 1 (you'll miss the dispersion signal); not noting miss direction; setting rings tighter than 9-iron radius (defeats the calibration to Shaw's benchmark).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, MSc, PGA Pro (Golf Insider UK)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-chipping-practice-lower-your-scores/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Tailor's Pitching Challenge$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 5 pitches from 15–20 yards and sum the total feet to the pin — Will Shaw's challenge is to get the cumulative under 30 feet, an average inside 6 feet per shot.$drill$,
 15, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Will Shaw's Tailor's Pitching Challenge swaps point-based scoring for the rawer measurement that actually predicts up-and-down conversion: distance from the hole. Pick a pin 15 to 20 yards away; hit 5 pitch shots; walk up and measure (or pace) each ball's distance to the pin in feet; sum the five distances. Shaw's pass mark is **under 30 feet total** — meaning an average of 6 feet or closer — which is roughly Tour-level proximity from that distance. A 50-foot total tells you your average pitch leaves a 10-footer; a 100-foot total flags a strike or distance problem you can't see from "they kind of go that way." The total-feet metric also resists the rounding-up bias that comes with binary in/out scoring — every foot you tighten dispersion shows up directly. **Who it helps most.** Casual players who can already make solid contact and need to learn that "around the hole" varies wildly when measured; developing/competitive players auditing scoring proximity. **Setup.** Short-game green with a flag; 15–20 yards of room; pitch wedge or 56°; 5 balls; a yardage measurer or rangefinder (or just pace). **How.** Hit 5 pitches from one consistent spot (don't drop a closer ball if one comes up short). Walk to each, measure feet to the pin, write it down. Sum the five; the total is your score. Beat the total next session. Tour-level reference: under 30 ft total. Note where misses miss — short, long, left, right. **Time & frequency.** 15 minutes weekly during a wedge block. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: under 60 ft total. • Developing: under 40 ft. • Competitive: under 30 ft (Shaw's pass mark). **Common mistakes.** Walking to the closest ball first and being "encouraged" — measure them all honestly; rounding down; rotating distances within the 5-ball set; hitting until you get a good one (the 5 you hit is the score).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{distance_control,chip_contact}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Will Shaw, PhD, MSc, PGA Pro (Golf Insider UK)$drill$, 'https://golfinsideruk.com/golf-chipping-practice-lower-your-scores/', 'oga', true),

($drill$15 Chips Game$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 5 easy, 5 medium, and 5 hard chips around the green — 1 point per ball within two club-lengths of the hole, bonus for a chip-in — and chase a higher score next session.$drill$,
 20, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Luke Benoit (Director of Instruction at Interlachen Country Club in Edina, Minnesota), writing in Golf Digest's "15 Best Practice Games" feature, structures his short-game scoring game around three levels of difficulty rather than three distances. Pick a routine easy chip from the fringe; pick a medium-difficulty chip from light rough or a slightly awkward lie; pick a hard chip — short-sided, bad lie, or downhill. Hit 5 from each (15 total). For every ball that finishes within **two club-lengths** of the hole, you earn 1 point; a holed chip earns a bonus point (OGA convention: +1 extra for a chip-in, per the article's "made chips receive bonus points" rule). The 2-club-length zone is wider than Tour proximity scoring — Benoit's framing is "anywhere you'd be confident with the next putt" — and the gradient of difficulty across the three rounds maps to what you actually face on the course. Max score is 15 (+ chip-in bonuses); track it across sessions and chase the trend, not any one number. **Who it helps most.** Casual and developing players who want to expose which difficulties they own and which they avoid. Competitive players use it as variety calibration. **Setup.** Short-game green with multiple lies and pin angles; pick 3 spots in advance — easy, medium, hard — and write them down so you can replay the same test next session. Chipping wedge, putter, 15 balls. Scorecard. **How.** Round 1: 5 chips from the easy spot; score each (+1 within 2 club-lengths, +1 extra chip-in). Round 2: medium. Round 3: hard. Sum to /15-plus-bonuses. Replay the same three spots next session and chase a higher total. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes weekly. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 6+ of 15. • Developing: 10+ of 15; the hard round above 3. • Competitive: 12+; the hard round 4+. **Common mistakes.** Picking three similar-difficulty spots; switching the spots session to session (kills comparison); only counting holed chips (the 2-club-length zone is the metric); skipping the hard round.$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{chip_contact,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Luke Benoit, Director of Instruction at Interlachen CC (Golf Digest / Australian Golf Digest)$drill$, 'https://www.australiangolfdigest.com.au/the-15-best-golf-practice-games/', 'oga', true),

($drill$6-Foot Circle Chip Score$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 50 chips from 15 yards — +2 for each ball inside a 6-foot circle, −1 outside, then putt every ball with +3 for makes and −1 for misses — and chase the 250-point ceiling.$drill$,
 25, 'around_green', '{short_game,putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Nick Foy's chipping scoring game stacks two metrics into one number: chip proximity AND putt conversion. From 15 yards off the green, hit 50 chips (five sets of 10). Each chip that finishes inside a 6-foot circle around the hole scores +2; each chip outside scores −1. Then putt every ball: a holed putt is +3, a missed putt is −1. The maximum is 250 (50 × 2 chip + 50 × 3 putt = 250); Foy calls **above 200 "professional level."** The combined chip-and-putt scoring fixes the single biggest hole in chipping practice — players who chip well to a 6-foot zone but can't roll the next putt in are not actually saving strokes on the course. The penalty for missed putts also keeps you honest under the chip-to-3-feet shortcut: a chip that lands closer makes the putt easier and produces a higher combined score. **Who it helps most.** Every level. Casual players use the chip score in isolation; developing/competitive players run the full chip-plus-putt for the real up-and-down benchmark. **Setup.** Short-game green with a flag and rollable surface; 15-yard chip distance from one consistent spot; tape, tees, or a measuring string to mark a 6-ft circle around the hole; chipping wedge and putter; 50 balls (chip + retrieve in batches of 10 if you don't have 50). Scorecard. **How.** Set 1: hit 10 chips from 15 yds, score each chip (+2 inside circle / −1 outside), then putt each ball (+3 made / −1 missed). Repeat 4 more sets of 10. Sum to /250. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes weekly during a short-game block. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: 100+ of 250. • Developing: 150+. • Competitive: 200+ (Foy's pro-level threshold). **Common mistakes.** Skipping the putt (halves the data); resetting the circle bigger because chips don't land in it (the circle is the standard); not tracking the chip and putt scores separately (the diagnostic value is in seeing which side leaks); only running one or two sets instead of all five (50 is the benchmark sample size).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80}', '{chip_contact,distance_control,lag}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Nick Foy (Foy Golf Academy)$drill$, 'https://nickfoygolf.com/chipping-practice-routine-5-golf-drills/', 'oga', true),

($drill$US National Junior Team Bunker Test$drill$,
 $drill$Hit 15 bunker shots, measure each in inches to the pin (200-inch cap for misses), throw out the highest 3, sum the remaining 12 — lower is better, beat your number.$drill$,
 25, 'around_green', '{short_game}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** The U.S. National Junior Team's bunker proximity test (head coach Chris Zambri; assistant coach Tiffany Joh, who runs the pin position from inside the green) measures what actually matters in bunker play: how close, on average, you finish — not whether you make the once-a-month hole-out. Hit 15 bunker shots from one consistent lie. After each, measure (or pace) the distance to the pin in inches; cap any genuine miss at 200 inches. Throw out the worst three numbers (the outliers — a chunk, a thin, a flyer). Sum the remaining 12. That total is your score; the lower it is, the better your bunker play, and you chase a lower total next session. The drop-the-worst-3 rule keeps a single bad swing from torching the diagnostic — Tour players have bad bunker shots too. The 200-inch (~17-foot) cap normalizes truly bad misses so they don't dominate the sum at the expense of the 14 close shots. The result is a stable, replayable number that's sensitive to real improvement and not to one outlier. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players auditing bunker proximity; juniors and amateurs who want a real Tour-format benchmark. Not for true beginners — needs reliable contact. **Setup.** A practice bunker; one consistent lie; a rangefinder or a yardage measure (paces converted to inches works); 15 balls. Scorecard with 15 cells. **How.** Hit 15 bunker shots from the same spot to the same flag. After each ball, measure feet-to-pin and convert to inches (or cap at 200 inches if the shot finishes longer than 17 ft / barely on the green / off the green). Write down all 15 numbers. Cross out the three highest. Sum the remaining 12 — that's your score. Beat the total next session. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes weekly during a bunker block; one of the few drills that gets sharper as conditions vary. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: under 1,500 inches (avg ~125"/10.4 ft per scoring ball). • Competitive: under 1,000 inches (avg ~80"/6.7 ft). • Tour reference: U.S. Junior Team members regularly post sub-700. **Common mistakes.** Eyeballing instead of measuring; measuring only the close ones; forgetting to cap the genuine misses (one chunk dominates the sum); throwing out fewer than 3 (or more) — the rule is exactly 3; rotating lies within a set (the drill needs one lie per session to be comparable).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{bunker,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Chris Zambri (head coach), Tiffany Joh (assistant) — U.S. National Junior Team (Golf.com)$drill$, 'https://golf.com/instruction/how-practice-like-us-national-junior-team/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Lag-It Points Game$drill$,
 $drill$Lay a club 18–24 inches past the hole and putt from any lag distance — 2 points for a make, 1 for a putt past the hole but short of the club, 0 for short or hitting the club.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{casual,developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Andy Hilts (VP of Instruction & Education at GolfTEC) built Lag-It around a single insight: amateur three-putts almost always come from being short, not long. The fix is a physical target that rewards rolling the ball past the hole but inside a controlled zone. Place a golf club on the green 18 to 24 inches past the hole. Now lag from any distance you want to train — 30, 40, 50 feet. Score every putt: a made putt is 2 points; a putt that finishes past the hole but doesn't reach (or hit) the club is 1 point; a putt left short of the hole is 0; a putt that hits the club or rolls past it is 0. The point values bias your speed control upward — the optimal lag finishes 6 to 24 inches past the hole, exactly the range Pelz's data identifies as the highest-conversion zone — and the +1 zone is wide enough that you can train at multiple distances in one session. Total your points across however many lag distances you choose; that total is your score-to-beat. **Who it helps most.** Every level — three-putt avoidance is the single biggest scoring leak for casual and developing players. Competitive players run it as pre-round speed calibration. **Setup.** A practice green with a hole; a golf club; balls (10 per distance is a reasonable sample); a scorecard. Pick 3–4 lag distances (e.g., 30, 40, 50 ft) — write them down. **How.** Lay the club 18–24" past the hole, perpendicular to your line. Putt the first distance (10 balls, score each), then move to the next distance, and so on. Sum points across all stations. Beat your total next session. The bias toward +1 (past but not past the club) trains you to never leave a lag short. **Time & frequency.** 20 minutes 2×/week during a lag block; 10 minutes pre-round as speed calibration. **Success metric, by level.** • Casual: positive total, with no zeros from being short. • Developing: 2+ makes per distance, most others scoring 1. • Competitive: 3+ makes from 30 ft; almost no short leaves. **Common mistakes.** Putting from one distance only (defeats the lag calibration); moving the club closer (raises the make rate but loses the "no-short" training); not tracking which distance produced the short putts (the actionable pattern); jogging the ball to the hole at the end (leave it to read the result).$drill$,
 '{break_100,break_90,break_80,break_70s}', '{lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Andy Hilts, VP Instruction & Education at GolfTEC$drill$, 'https://scramble.golftec.com/blog/2014/11/lag-putting-game/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Hole 100 Feet of Putts$drill$,
 $drill$Hit one 5, 10, 15, and 20-foot putt to each of 5 different hole locations — 20 putts, 250 feet possible — and add up the total footage of putts holed. Tour level is 100 feet.$drill$,
 25, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Phil Kenyon (putting coach to multiple major winners; quoted in Today's Golfer) scores his pressure-free putting test by aggregate footage of holed putts, not make count. Pick a hole on the practice green; hit one putt each at 5, 10, 15, and 20 feet using your full pre-shot routine. Now move to a different hole location with a different angle of break, and repeat the four putts. Repeat across 5 hole locations total — 20 putts, 250 feet of possible footage. After each holed putt, tally the distance: a 20-footer that drops is +20 feet, a 5-footer that drops is +5 feet. Misses count 0 regardless of how close. Sum the holed footage. Kenyon's PGA Tour winner benchmark is **100 feet of holed putts out of 250 possible**; the all-time best he's recorded is Henrik Stenson at 145. The footage-weighting matters: a player making lots of 5-footers but no long ones scores low; a player who mixes in a 20-footer climbs fast. The 5-hole rotation eliminates "I just keep making this one putt" — you read 5 different breaks, 5 different angles, on every distance. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players who already convert short putts and need to test the full distance band. Not for casual players — the long putts will dominate the score uselessly without baseline lag skill. **Setup.** Practice green with multiple hole locations and break variety; 4 balls; tees or markers at 5/10/15/20 ft from each hole; scorecard. **How.** From Hole 1: hit your 5-footer (routine), then 10, 15, 20 ft. Record whether each dropped and the holed footage. Move to Hole 2 (different angle/break), repeat. Total 5 holes × 4 putts = 20 putts. Sum holed footage. Beat next session; chase the 100-ft Tour benchmark. **Time & frequency.** 25 minutes weekly. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 40+ ft holed. • Competitive: 70+ ft. • Tour reference: 100 ft = Tour winner level (Kenyon's bar). **Common mistakes.** Same hole all 20 putts (loses the read variety); skipping the routine; counting "close" misses; chasing the 20-footers and skimping on the 5s (the math doesn't work — every 5-footer made = the 5-footer; every 5-footer missed = 0).$drill$,
 '{break_90,break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,lag,distance_control,green_reading}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Phil Kenyon, putting coach (Today's Golfer)$drill$, 'https://www.todays-golfer.com/features/instruction-features/2020/september/phil-kenyon-golf-putting-tips/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Putting Skill Test (18-Putt)$drill$,
 $drill$Three putts each from six distance bands (3–5 / 5–10 / 10–15 / 15–20 / 20–25 / 25–30 ft) = 18 birdie putts. Made = 1 stroke, missed = 2 (tap-in). Total vs par 72.$drill$,
 30, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Bradley Turner (PGA Certified Professional; Executive Director, Keiser University College of Golf) frames an 18-putt test as if you've hit every green in regulation — 18 birdie putts spread across the six distance bands you actually face. Hit 3 putts each from 3–5, 5–10, 10–15, 15–20, 20–25, and 25–30 feet (18 total). Each made putt is a birdie (1 stroke); each missed putt is treated as a tap-in two-putt (2 strokes). Sum to your simulated 18-hole putting round; subtract from par 72 for an under-par score. The PGA Tour average works out to **roughly 66.1** based on Tour conversion percentages and the typical 0.50 three-putts per round — meaning a Tour player rolling this test would shoot about 6 under. Scratch amateurs land near 67–68; mid-handicaps closer to 70–71. The structure is what makes it a real benchmark: it samples the full putting distance spectrum a round actually demands, in proportion, and the par-72 scoring is intuitive and comparable across sessions. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players auditing total putting strokes versus Tour benchmarks; players prepping for a Pelz-style structured test without the 80-shot scale. **Setup.** Practice green with multiple hole locations and variable break; tees or markers at 3 putts per band × 6 bands = 18 stations; scorecard. (Spread stations across the green so you don't rep the same break repeatedly.) **How.** From each band, hit 3 putts to 3 different hole locations within that distance window. Record each: made = 1, missed = 2 (no third putt — assume tap-in). Sum to your 18-hole total; subtract from par 72 for your under-par score. **Time & frequency.** 30 minutes monthly as a benchmark — not weekly; the variance per session is high and you want session-to-session signal. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: par or 1–2 under. • Competitive: 3–5 under (68–69 raw). • Tour benchmark: 6 under (66.1, Turner's published Tour avg). **Common mistakes.** Putting 18 putts to the same hole (no read variety); skipping the missed-putt = 2 scoring (under-counts strokes); running it weekly (high variance, low signal); counting close misses (the rule is hard make or miss).$drill$,
 '{break_80,break_70s,scratch}', '{short_putts,lag,distance_control}', 'skill_game',
 $drill$Bradley Turner, PGA Certified Professional, Executive Director — Keiser University College of Golf$drill$, 'https://collegeofgolf.keiseruniversity.edu/test-your-putting-skill/', 'oga', true),

($drill$Makes Drill$drill$,
 $drill$Five balls each at 3, 4, 11, and 12 feet around the same hole — 20 putts total, simply count makes. PGA Tour pro Sam Ryder's go-to short-putt scoring game.$drill$,
 20, 'putting', '{putting}', '{developing,competitive}',
 $drill$**Why it works.** Sam Ryder (PGA Tour player; coached interview in Golf.com by Brendon R. Elliott, PGA) keeps his scoring game radically simple — count makes — because the simplicity is what makes it replayable enough to compete against. Place 5 balls at 3 feet from the hole (around the circle, so each is a different break); putt them all. Move to 4 feet; putt all 5. Then 11 feet; then 12 feet. 20 putts total. Total makes is your score; "keep track of how many putts you make, and you can compete against yourself or friends to get the highest score possible." The 3 and 4-foot work locks in the must-make putts; the 11 and 12-foot work attacks the band where amateurs lose the most strokes per round (the make rate falls off a cliff from 6 to 12 feet). The combination makes it both a confidence drill and a real diagnostic — a player making 10 of 10 inside 4 ft but only 1 of 10 outside 10 ft is doing one job and not the other. Ryder runs this regularly during Tour weeks; the simplicity is the feature. **Who it helps most.** Developing and competitive players. Casual players will get crushed by the 11–12 ft band; better to drill 3–4 ft alone first. **Setup.** Practice green with a hole; tees or markers at 3, 4, 11, 12 ft (5 markers per distance around the hole — vary the break direction at each); 5 balls per distance; scorecard. **How.** Start at 3 ft: hit all 5 putts, count makes. Move to 4 ft: 5 putts, count makes. 11 ft: 5 putts. 12 ft: 5 putts. Total /20. Beat your score next session. To add a focused short-band variant, run only 3 and 4 ft (10 putts, /10). **Time & frequency.** 15–20 minutes 2–3×/week — quick enough to slot into any session. **Success metric, by level.** • Developing: 10+ of 20 (inside 4 ft near-perfect; ~1–2 makes outside 10 ft). • Competitive: 14+ of 20; inside 4 ft = perfect; 3–4 makes outside 10 ft. • Tour reference: Tour avg from 11–12 ft is ~25–30%, so ~3 of 10 outside is solid. **Common mistakes.** Putting all 5 from the same line (loses the break variety the circle gives); not running the long band (the 11–12 ft work is the leverage); skipping the short band when bored (the 3-footers ARE the score, on a course); not recording the score (no benchmark to chase).$drill$,
